window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-breathing","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases · Practice","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The structure an earthworm uses for exchange of gases is its:","image":"","options":["Network of tracheal tubes","Pair of vascularised lungs","Moist cuticle","Gills present on the body segments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that earthworms use their moist cuticle for gas exchange.\nThe moisture lets gases diffuse across the body covering.\nThe tracheal tubes option is the tempting one, but tracheal tubes are the insect system and earthworms do not have them.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A person breathing quietly stops at the end of a normal expiration. He then inhales as forcefully as possible and immediately exhales as forcefully as possible. Which option correctly gives (i) the volume inhaled during the forced inhalation and (ii) the volume left in the lungs at the end of the forced exhalation?","image":"","options":["(i) Inspiratory capacity; (ii) Residual volume","(i) Vital capacity; (ii) Residual volume","(i) Inspiratory capacity; (ii) Functional residual capacity","(i) Vital capacity; (ii) Functional residual capacity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) The inhalation starts from the end of a normal expiration, not a forced one. So the air drawn in is TV + IRV, the inspiratory capacity. VC would require starting after a forced expiration.\n(ii) A forcible expiration empties the lungs as far as possible, but air still remains. This is the residual volume. FRC is what remains after a normal expiration only.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In humans, the trachea extends up to the mid-thoracic cavity. At the level of which vertebra does it divide into the right and left primary bronchi?","image":"","options":["3rd thoracic vertebra","5th thoracic vertebra","7th thoracic vertebra","5th cervical vertebra"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the trachea as a straight tube that extends up to the mid-thoracic cavity.\nIt divides into the right and left primary bronchi at the level of the 5th thoracic vertebra.\nThe 5th cervical vertebra is a tempting distractor because the number matches. It is wrong because it lies in the neck, well above the mid-thoracic cavity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A spirometer record shows a vital capacity of 4500 mL, a tidal volume of 500 mL and an expiratory reserve volume of 1000 mL. What is the inspiratory reserve volume?","image":"","options":["2500 mL","3000 mL","3500 mL","4000 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: VC = 4500 mL, TV = 500 mL, ERV = 1000 mL.\nFormula: VC = ERV + TV + IRV, so IRV = VC − TV − ERV.\nSubstituting: IRV = 4500 − 500 − 1000.\nSo IRV = 3000 mL, which falls in the NCERT range of 2500–3000 mL.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"By which process are $O_2$ and $CO_2$ exchanged between alveoli and blood, and between blood and tissues?","image":"","options":["Active transport using ATP","Simple diffusion along a pressure/concentration gradient","Carrier-mediated facilitated diffusion","Osmosis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says $O_2$ and $CO_2$ are exchanged at both sites by simple diffusion.\nThis diffusion depends mainly on the pressure (concentration) gradient.\nNo energy or carrier protein is needed, so active transport and facilitated diffusion are wrong.\nOsmosis is the movement of water, not of gases.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The lungs are set in the thorax in such a way that any change in the volume of the thoracic cavity is reflected in the pulmonary cavity, and this arrangement is essential for breathing.\nReason (R): Humans cannot directly alter the pulmonary volume.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The lungs lie in an air-tight thoracic chamber, so changes in thoracic volume are passed on to the lungs.\nR is true. The lungs have no means of directly changing their own volume.\nNCERT states that the arrangement is essential precisely because pulmonary volume cannot be altered directly. Muscles change thoracic volume, and this in turn changes lung volume.\nSo R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The thoracic chamber is anatomically an air-tight chamber.\n(ii) Humans can directly alter pulmonary volume without changing thoracic volume.\n(iii) During inspiration, a negative pressure exists in the lungs with respect to atmospheric pressure.\n(iv) Normal expiration requires active contraction of the diaphragm.\n(v) Additional abdominal muscles can increase the strength of both inspiration and expiration.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT describes the thoracic chamber as anatomically air-tight.\n(ii) Incorrect: we cannot directly alter pulmonary volume. It changes only because thoracic volume changes.\n(iii) Correct: intra-pulmonary pressure falls below atmospheric pressure during inspiration.\n(iv) Incorrect: normal expiration results from relaxation of the diaphragm, not its contraction.\n(v) Correct: abdominal muscles can strengthen both phases of breathing.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three major layers of the alveolar diffusion membrane?","image":"","options":["Thin squamous epithelium of the alveoli","Endothelium of the alveolar capillaries","Basement substance between the epithelium and the endothelium","Smooth muscle layer of the bronchiole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The diffusion membrane has three layers.\nThese are the alveolar squamous epithelium, the capillary endothelium and the basement substance between them.\nThe basement substance is made of two basement membranes.\nA smooth muscle layer is not part of this membrane, and a thick muscular layer would hinder diffusion.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Most aquatic arthropods and molluscs use special vascularised structures called gills for exchange of gases.\nStatement II: Among vertebrates, gills are found in fishes as well as in reptiles.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that most aquatic arthropods and molluscs use vascularised gills (branchial respiration).\nStatement II is incorrect. Among vertebrates only fishes use gills. Reptiles, even aquatic ones, respire through lungs, as do amphibians, birds and mammals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A fall in blood $O_2$ level is the major factor regulating the respiratory rhythm in humans.\nStatement II: The chemosensitive area that helps adjust breathing is situated adjacent to the respiratory rhythm centre.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. NCERT says the role of oxygen in regulating respiratory rhythm is quite insignificant. Changes in $CO_2$ and $H^+$ are the key signals.\nStatement II is true. The chemosensitive area lies adjacent to the rhythm centre in the medulla.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Binding of oxygen with haemoglobin is primarily related to the partial pressure of CO$_2$.\nStatement II: Oxygen binds with haemoglobin in a reversible manner to form oxyhaemoglobin.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. O$_2$ binding is primarily related to pO$_2$.\npCO$_2$, H$^+$ concentration and temperature are only other factors that can interfere with this binding.\nStatement II is correct. The binding is reversible, which is why O$_2$ is picked up in the lungs and released at the tissues.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which structure forms the lower side (floor) of the air-tight thoracic chamber that houses the lungs?","image":"","options":["Sternum","Vertebral column","Dome-shaped diaphragm","Ribs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The thoracic chamber is bounded dorsally by the vertebral column, ventrally by the sternum and laterally by the ribs.\nThe dome-shaped diaphragm forms its lower side.\nThe sternum is a tempting choice, but it closes the chamber from the front, not from below.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A doctor wants to estimate the volumes of air a patient moves during breathing movements, as part of a clinical assessment of pulmonary function. Which instrument is used?","image":"","options":["Sphygmomanometer","Spirometer","Stethoscope","Electrocardiograph"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the volume of air involved in breathing movements is estimated with a spirometer.\nThis helps in the clinical assessment of pulmonary functions.\nA sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure.\nA stethoscope is used to listen to body sounds.\nAn electrocardiograph records the electrical activity of the heart.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Use the NCERT partial pressure values for alveoli, deoxygenated blood, oxygenated blood and tissues to evaluate the two statements below.\nStatement I: At the alveolar–capillary interface, the $pO_2$ gradient (alveoli to deoxygenated blood) is 64 mm Hg, whereas the $pCO_2$ gradient (deoxygenated blood to alveoli) is only 5 mm Hg.\nStatement II: The $pO_2$ gradient from oxygenated blood to tissues is greater than the $pO_2$ gradient from alveoli to deoxygenated blood.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I, $O_2$ at the lungs: 104 − 40 = 64 mm Hg.\nStatement I, $CO_2$ at the lungs: 45 − 40 = 5 mm Hg. So Statement I is correct.\nStatement II, $O_2$ at the tissues: 95 − 40 = 55 mm Hg. This is smaller than 64 mm Hg, so Statement II is incorrect.\nNote: $CO_2$ still diffuses efficiently despite its small 5 mm Hg gradient, because it is 20–25 times more soluble than $O_2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about partial pressures is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$pCO_2$ of atmospheric air (0.3 mm Hg) is far lower than that of alveolar air","$pO_2$ of tissues is equal to $pO_2$ of deoxygenated blood","$pCO_2$ of deoxygenated blood is lower than $pCO_2$ of alveolar air","$pO_2$ of oxygenated blood is higher than $pO_2$ of tissues"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: atmospheric $pCO_2$ is 0.3 mm Hg, while alveolar $pCO_2$ is 40 mm Hg.\n(2) Correct: both tissues and deoxygenated blood have a $pO_2$ of 40 mm Hg.\n(3) Incorrect: deoxygenated blood has a $pCO_2$ of 45 mm Hg, which is higher than the alveolar 40 mm Hg. This is why $CO_2$ moves from blood into the alveoli.\n(4) Correct: oxygenated blood has a $pO_2$ of 95 mm Hg, higher than the tissue value of 40 mm Hg.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (animal) with List-II (respiratory surface/organ):\nList-I:\nA. Earthworm\nB. Cockroach\nC. Prawn\nD. Planaria\nList-II:\nI. Gills\nII. Tracheal tubes\nIII. Diffusion over entire body surface\nIV. Moist cuticle\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A. Earthworm uses its moist cuticle, so A-IV.\nB. Cockroach is an insect with tracheal tubes, so B-II.\nC. Prawn is an aquatic arthropod that uses gills, so C-I.\nD. Planaria is a flatworm that relies on diffusion over the entire body surface, so D-III.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Normal quiet expiration in humans occurs without contraction of the diaphragm.\nReason (R): Relaxation of the diaphragm and intercostal muscles reduces thoracic and pulmonary volume, raising intra-pulmonary pressure slightly above atmospheric pressure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: quiet expiration is passive. It needs only relaxation of the muscles, not contraction of the diaphragm.\nR is true: when the muscles relax, the diaphragm and sternum return to their normal positions. Thoracic volume falls, so pulmonary volume falls too.\nThe fall in volume raises intra-pulmonary pressure slightly above atmospheric pressure, and air is pushed out.\nR therefore explains why expiration happens without diaphragm contraction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The volume of air that still remains in the lungs even after a forcible expiration is called:","image":"","options":["Expiratory reserve volume","Residual volume","Tidal volume","Inspiratory reserve volume"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines residual volume (RV) as the air left in the lungs even after a forcible expiration. It averages 1100 to 1200 mL.\nExpiratory reserve volume is the tempting wrong choice. ERV is the extra air a person can push out by forcible expiration, so it leaves the lungs. RV is the air that stays behind.\nTidal volume and IRV relate to normal breathing and forced inspiration.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In workers of stone-breaking industries, long exposure to dust can cause fibrosis of the lungs. Fibrosis refers to","image":"","options":["proliferation of fibrous tissues","inflammation of the bronchioles","rupture of the alveolar walls","collapse of the trachea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Long exposure to industrial dust causes inflammation that leads to fibrosis.\nNCERT explains fibrosis as the proliferation (excessive growth) of fibrous tissues, which causes serious lung damage.\nInflammation of the bronchioles describes asthma.\nRupture or damage of the alveolar walls describes emphysema.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Terminal bronchioles belong to the conducting part of the respiratory system and are not sites of gas exchange.\nStatement II: The conducting part humidifies the inhaled air, clears it of foreign particles and brings it to body temperature.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The conducting part runs from the external nostrils up to the terminal bronchioles. Gas exchange happens only in the alveoli and their ducts, which form the exchange part.\nStatement II is correct. Besides carrying air to the alveoli, the conducting part filters, humidifies and warms the air to body temperature.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (site) with List-II (pO$_2$ in mm Hg):\nList-I\n(A) Atmospheric air\n(B) Alveoli\n(C) Oxygenated blood\n(D) Tissues\nList-II\n(i) 40\n(ii) 95\n(iii) 104\n(iv) 159","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"From the NCERT table of partial pressures:\nAtmospheric air pO$_2$ = 159 mm Hg, so A-iv.\nAlveoli = 104 mm Hg, so B-iii.\nOxygenated blood = 95 mm Hg, so C-ii.\nTissues = 40 mm Hg, so D-i.\nThis falling gradient drives O$_2$ from the alveoli to the blood and from the blood to the tissues.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about exchange of gases are correct?\n(i) $O_2$ and $CO_2$ are exchanged at the alveoli and at the tissues by simple diffusion.\n(ii) The total thickness of the alveolar diffusion membrane is more than one millimetre.\n(iii) At the lungs, the $pCO_2$ gradient runs from the alveoli to the blood.\n(iv) The solubility of a gas affects its rate of diffusion.\n(v) Gas exchange also occurs through the walls of the terminal bronchioles.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: exchange at both sites occurs by simple diffusion along pressure gradients.\n(ii) Incorrect: the total thickness of the membrane is much less than a millimetre.\n(iii) Incorrect: alveolar $pCO_2$ is 40 mm Hg and deoxygenated blood is 45 mm Hg, so the gradient runs from blood to alveoli.\n(iv) Correct: solubility, partial pressure gradient and membrane thickness all affect diffusion.\n(v) Incorrect: terminal bronchioles belong to the conducting part, not the exchange part.\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Oxygen dissociates from oxyhaemoglobin at the body tissues.\nReason (R): The tissues have low pO$_2$, high pCO$_2$, high H$^+$ concentration and higher temperature.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: O$_2$ binds to haemoglobin at the lung surface and is released at the tissues.\nR is true: NCERT lists exactly these conditions for the tissues.\nThese very conditions are what favour dissociation of oxyhaemoglobin, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy adult has a tidal volume of 500 mL and an inspiratory reserve volume of 3000 mL. What is the inspiratory capacity?","image":"","options":["2500 mL","3000 mL","3500 mL","4000 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: TV = 500 mL, IRV = 3000 mL.\nFormula: Inspiratory capacity IC = TV + IRV. This is the total air a person can inspire after a normal expiration.\nSubstituting: IC = 500 + 3000.\nSo IC = 3500 mL.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) 97 per cent\n(b) 3 per cent\n(c) 4\n(d) 5 mL\nList-II\n(i) Maximum number of O$_2$ molecules carried by one haemoglobin molecule\n(ii) O$_2$ delivered to tissues by 100 mL of oxygenated blood under normal conditions\n(iii) Share of O$_2$ transported by RBCs\n(iv) Share of O$_2$ carried dissolved in plasma","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) 97% of O$_2$ is carried by RBCs, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) 3% of O$_2$ is dissolved in plasma, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Each haemoglobin molecule carries a maximum of 4 O$_2$ molecules, so (c)-(i).\n(d) Every 100 mL of oxygenated blood delivers about 5 mL of O$_2$ to tissues, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In insects, atmospheric air is transported within the body through:","image":"","options":["Book lungs that open on the ventral surface","Gills richly supplied with blood vessels","The moist body wall by simple diffusion","A network of tubes called tracheal tubes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that insects have a network of tubes, called tracheal tubes, that carry atmospheric air within the body.\nSimple diffusion over the body surface is the method of lower invertebrates such as sponges and flatworms, not insects.\nGills are used by aquatic arthropods, not by terrestrial insects.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Respiratory rhythm centre\nB. Pneumotaxic centre\nC. Chemosensitive area\nD. Aortic arch and carotid artery receptors\nList-II\n(i) Located in pons; can reduce duration of inspiration\n(ii) Located in medulla; primarily responsible for regulating rhythm\n(iii) Adjacent to rhythm centre; activated by rise in $CO_2$ and $H^+$\n(iv) Detect $CO_2$ and $H^+$ changes outside the brain and signal rhythm centre","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-(ii): The rhythm centre lies in the medulla and is primarily responsible for regulating rhythm.\nB-(i): The pneumotaxic centre lies in the pons and shortens inspiration.\nC-(iii): The chemosensitive area lies adjacent to the rhythm centre and responds to $CO_2$ and $H^+$.\nD-(iv): The aortic arch and carotid artery receptors detect $CO_2$ and $H^+$ changes and signal the rhythm centre.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following belong to the conducting part of the human respiratory system?\nExternal nostrils, pharynx, trachea, terminal bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveoli","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines the conducting part as everything from the external nostrils up to the terminal bronchioles.\nConducting: external nostrils, pharynx, trachea and terminal bronchioles, which makes 4.\nExchange part: alveolar ducts and alveoli, where O2 and CO2 actually diffuse.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The larynx is a cartilaginous box that helps in sound production and is therefore called the sound box.\nStatement II: During swallowing, the glottis is covered by the epiglottis, a thin elastic cartilaginous flap that prevents food from entering the larynx.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT describes the larynx as a cartilaginous box that helps in sound production, so it is called the sound box.\nStatement II is correct. During swallowing, the epiglottis, a thin elastic cartilaginous flap, covers the glottis and stops food from entering the larynx.\nBoth statements agree with the text.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Regarding the enzyme carbonic anhydrase in human blood, which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["It is present only in plasma and absent from RBCs","It is present in very high concentration in RBCs and in minute quantities in plasma","It is present in equal concentrations in RBCs and plasma","It is present only in RBCs and absent from plasma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that RBCs contain a very high concentration of carbonic anhydrase.\nMinute quantities of the enzyme are also present in plasma.\nSo the enzyme is neither confined to one compartment nor equally distributed.\nThe tempting option 'only in RBCs' fails because plasma does contain small amounts.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about transport of gases in human blood are correct?\n(i) About 97 per cent of O$_2$ is transported by RBCs.\n(ii) About 70 per cent of CO$_2$ is carried as bicarbonate.\n(iii) About 20-25 per cent of CO$_2$ is carried dissolved in plasma.\n(iv) One haemoglobin molecule can carry at most four O$_2$ molecules.\n(v) Carbonic anhydrase is present in very high concentration in plasma and in minute quantity in RBCs.\n(vi) Every 100 mL of deoxygenated blood delivers about 4 mL of CO$_2$ to the alveoli.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the remaining 3 per cent of O$_2$ is dissolved in plasma.\n(ii) Correct: about 70 per cent of CO$_2$ travels as bicarbonate.\n(iii) Incorrect: only about 7 per cent of CO$_2$ is dissolved in plasma. The 20-25 per cent figure is for carbamino-haemoglobin.\n(iv) Correct: each haemoglobin carries a maximum of four O$_2$.\n(v) Incorrect: the distribution is the reverse. RBCs have a very high concentration of carbonic anhydrase and plasma has minute amounts.\n(vi) Correct.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"During vigorous exercise, $CO_2$ and $H^+$ accumulate in the blood. Which sequence correctly describes how the body corrects this, according to NCERT?","image":"","options":["Pneumotaxic centre activated → lengthens inspiration → chemosensitive area inhibited → $CO_2$ retained","Aortic and carotid receptors detect low $O_2$ → signal pneumotaxic centre → breathing slows","Chemosensitive area activated → signals respiratory rhythm centre → breathing adjusted → $CO_2$ and $H^+$ eliminated","Respiratory rhythm centre activated → signals chemosensitive area → breathing stops → $CO_2$ eliminated"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: Rising $CO_2$ and $H^+$ activate the chemosensitive area next to the rhythm centre.\nStep 2: The chemosensitive area signals the rhythm centre.\nStep 3: The rhythm centre adjusts breathing so the excess is eliminated.\nOption 1 is wrong: it reverses the logic and leaves $CO_2$ retained.\nOption 2 is wrong: it relies on $O_2$, whose role is insignificant.\nOption 4 is wrong: it reverses the direction of signalling.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (animal) with List-II (main means of gas exchange):\nList-I:\n(A) Sponge\n(B) Earthworm\n(C) Prawn (an aquatic arthropod)\n(D) Lizard\nList-II:\n(i) Gills (branchial respiration)\n(ii) Lungs (pulmonary respiration)\n(iii) Moist cuticle\n(iv) Simple diffusion over the entire body surface\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Sponge: lower invertebrates exchange gases by simple diffusion over the whole body surface, so A-(iv).\n(B) Earthworm: it uses its moist cuticle, so B-(iii).\n(C) Prawn: most aquatic arthropods use vascularised gills, so C-(i).\n(D) Lizard: reptiles, being terrestrial vertebrates, respire through lungs, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about $CO_2$ transport are correct?\n(i) At the tissues, $CO_2$ entering the blood forms $HCO_3^-$ and $H^+$.\n(ii) Carbonic anhydrase is completely absent from plasma.\n(iii) The largest fraction of $CO_2$ is carried as carbamino-haemoglobin.\n(iv) Low pCO2 and high pO2 in the alveoli favour dissociation of $CO_2$ from carbamino-haemoglobin.\n(v) About 7 per cent of $CO_2$ is carried dissolved in plasma.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: high pCO2 at the tissues drives the formation of $HCO_3^-$ and $H^+$.\n(ii) Incorrect: plasma contains minute quantities of carbonic anhydrase.\n(iii) Incorrect: the largest fraction, about 70 per cent, is carried as bicarbonate; carbamino-haemoglobin carries only 20-25 per cent.\n(iv) Correct: these alveolar conditions release $CO_2$ from haemoglobin.\n(v) Correct.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Blood flows from alveolar capillaries to capillaries in an actively contracting muscle. Which of the following correctly describes the events in the muscle capillaries?","image":"","options":["Low $H^+$ concentration and lower temperature promote dissociation of oxyhaemoglobin","Carbonic anhydrase drives the formation of $CO_2$ and $H_2O$ from $HCO_3^-$","High $pCO_2$ and low $pO_2$ favour formation of carbamino-haemoglobin, while $CO_2$ is converted to $HCO_3^-$ and $H^+$","Carbonic anhydrase acts only in plasma because RBCs lack this enzyme"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At the tissues, $pCO_2$ is high and $pO_2$ is low. These conditions favour $CO_2$ binding to haemoglobin as carbamino-haemoglobin, and carbonic anhydrase converts $CO_2$ to $HCO_3^-$ and $H^+$. So option (3) is correct.\nOption (1) is wrong: oxyhaemoglobin dissociates under high $H^+$ and higher temperature, not low.\nOption (2) is wrong: the reaction forming $CO_2$ and $H_2O$ occurs at the alveoli, where $pCO_2$ is low.\nOption (4) is wrong: RBCs have a very high concentration of carbonic anhydrase, and plasma has only minute amounts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Inspiratory capacity\n(b) Expiratory capacity\n(c) Functional residual capacity\n(d) Total lung capacity\nList-II\n(i) ERV + RV\n(ii) TV + ERV\n(iii) RV + ERV + TV + IRV\n(iv) TV + IRV","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i","a-i, b-ii, c-iv, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Inspiratory capacity is the air inspired after a normal expiration, so it equals TV + IRV (iv).\n(b) Expiratory capacity is the air expired after a normal inspiration, so it equals TV + ERV (ii).\n(c) Functional residual capacity is the air left after a normal expiration, so it equals ERV + RV (i).\n(d) Total lung capacity is the sum of all four volumes, RV + ERV + TV + IRV (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A person has the following respiratory volumes: tidal volume = 500 mL, inspiratory reserve volume = 3000 mL, expiratory reserve volume = 1100 mL and residual volume = 1200 mL. What volume of air is present in his lungs at the end of a normal (quiet) inspiration?","image":"","options":["1700 mL","2800 mL","3500 mL","5800 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: TV = 500, IRV = 3000, ERV = 1100, RV = 1200 (all in mL).\nAfter a normal expiration, the lungs still hold ERV + RV = 1100 + 1200 = 2300 mL.\nA normal inspiration then adds the tidal volume: 2300 + 500 = 2800 mL.\nIRV is not included, because no forcible inspiration occurs. The 5800 mL option is the total lung capacity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are supported by incomplete cartilaginous rings?\nTrachea, primary bronchi, secondary bronchi, tertiary bronchi, initial bronchioles, terminal bronchioles, alveoli","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, incomplete cartilaginous rings support the trachea, primary bronchi, secondary bronchi, tertiary bronchi and initial bronchioles. That makes 5 structures.\nTerminal bronchioles are very thin and have no rings.\nAlveoli are thin, irregular-walled sacs and also lack cartilage.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the pleura of human lungs is correct?","image":"","options":["The inner pleural membrane is in close contact with the thoracic lining","Pleural fluid is present inside the alveoli","The outer pleural membrane is in close contact with the thoracic lining","The pleura is a single layer that increases friction during breathing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each lung is covered by a double-layered pleura, with pleural fluid between the two layers.\nThe outer membrane lies against the thoracic lining, and the inner membrane lies against the lung surface. So option (3) is correct and option (1) reverses the two layers.\nPleural fluid lies between the membranes, not inside the alveoli, and it reduces friction rather than increasing it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Carbonic anhydrase catalyses only the formation of bicarbonate from $CO_2$ and water, and so it acts only at the tissue level.\nStatement II: In the tissues, both the high pCO2 and the low pO2 favour the binding of $CO_2$ to haemoglobin.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Carbonic anhydrase facilitates the reaction $CO_2 + H_2O \\rightleftharpoons H_2CO_3 \\rightleftharpoons HCO_3^- + H^+$ in both directions.\nIt forms bicarbonate at the tissues and regenerates $CO_2$ at the alveoli.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that high pCO2 and low pO2, as found in the tissues, lead to more binding of $CO_2$ as carbamino-haemoglobin.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In the human respiratory system, incomplete cartilaginous rings support several parts of the air passage. Which of the following is NOT supported by such rings?","image":"","options":["Trachea","Tertiary bronchi","Initial bronchioles","Alveoli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the trachea, the primary, secondary and tertiary bronchi, and the initial bronchioles are supported by incomplete cartilaginous rings.\nAlveoli are very thin, irregular-walled, vascularised bag-like structures. They carry no cartilage, which keeps them thin enough for diffusion.\nInitial bronchioles may look like a tempting wrong choice, but NCERT includes them among the supported parts.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The additional volume of air a person can inspire by a forcible inspiration averages about:","image":"","options":["500 mL","1000 to 1100 mL","1100 to 1200 mL","2500 to 3000 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The extra air taken in by a forcible inspiration is the inspiratory reserve volume (IRV).\nNCERT gives its average as 2500 to 3000 mL.\nThe other options belong to other volumes:\n- 500 mL is tidal volume.\n- 1000 to 1100 mL is ERV.\n- 1100 to 1200 mL is RV.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In human blood, what fraction of the total oxygen is carried in a dissolved state through the plasma?","image":"","options":["About 3 per cent","About 7 per cent","About 20-25 per cent","About 97 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that about 97% of O$_2$ is transported by RBCs.\nThe remaining 3% of O$_2$ is carried dissolved in plasma.\nThe value 7% is a common confusion. It is the share of CO$_2$, not O$_2$, carried dissolved in plasma.\nThe value 20-25% is the share of CO$_2$ carried as carbamino-haemoglobin.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Asthma mainly affects the conducting part of the respiratory system.\nStatement II: Emphysema mainly affects the respiratory (exchange) part of the respiratory system, which directly lowers the diffusion of O$_2$ and CO$_2$ between alveolar air and blood.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The conducting part extends from the external nostrils up to the terminal bronchioles. The exchange part consists of the alveoli and their ducts.\nStatement I is correct: asthma inflames the bronchi and bronchioles, which belong to the conducting part.\nStatement II is correct: emphysema damages the alveolar walls, which reduces the respiratory surface. Since the alveoli are the site of actual diffusion, gas exchange is directly impaired.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"On average, how many times per minute does a healthy human breathe at rest?","image":"","options":["6-8 times","20-30 times","72-75 times","12-16 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that a healthy human breathes on average 12-16 times per minute.\nThe value 72-75 is a common trap because it is the heart rate in beats per minute, not the breathing rate.\nThe values 6-8 and 20-30 lie outside the normal resting range given in the text.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following steps of respiration:\nP. Transport of gases by the blood\nQ. Diffusion of $O_2$ and $CO_2$ across the alveolar membrane\nR. Utilisation of $O_2$ by cells and release of $CO_2$\nS. Pulmonary ventilation (breathing)\nT. Diffusion of $O_2$ and $CO_2$ between blood and tissues\nChoose the correct sequence.","image":"","options":["S → Q → T → P → R","S → Q → P → T → R","Q → S → P → T → R","S → P → Q → T → R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Breathing draws in air and releases $CO_2$-rich alveolar air (S).\nStep 2: Gases diffuse across the alveolar membrane (Q).\nStep 3: Blood transports the gases (P).\nStep 4: Gases diffuse between blood and tissues (T).\nStep 5: Cells use $O_2$ in catabolic reactions and release $CO_2$ (R).\nThe correct sequence is therefore S → Q → P → T → R.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When 3 litres of oxygenated blood pass through the body tissues under normal physiological conditions, about 150 mL of O$_2$ is delivered to the tissues.\nStatement II: Binding of oxygen with haemoglobin is primarily related to the partial pressure of CO$_2$, while pO$_2$ only interferes with this binding.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: 100 mL of oxygenated blood delivers about 5 mL of O$_2$.\nSo 3000 mL delivers (3000/100) × 5 = 150 mL. Statement I is correct.\nStatement II: NCERT says O$_2$ binding to haemoglobin is primarily related to pO$_2$.\npCO$_2$, H$^+$ concentration and temperature are the factors that interfere with this binding.\nStatement II reverses these roles, so it is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Contraction of diaphragm\n(B) Contraction of external intercostal muscles\n(C) Relaxation of diaphragm and intercostal muscles\n(D) Muscles of the abdomen\nList-II\n(i) Increase in thoracic volume in the dorso-ventral axis\n(ii) Increase the strength of inspiration and expiration\n(iii) Increase in thoracic volume in the antero-posterior axis\n(iv) Intra-pulmonary pressure rises slightly above atmospheric pressure","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The diaphragm contracts and enlarges the thorax antero-posteriorly, so A matches (iii).\n(B) The external intercostals lift the ribs and sternum, giving a dorso-ventral increase, so B matches (i).\n(C) Muscle relaxation reduces volume, so intra-pulmonary pressure rises slightly above atmospheric, so C matches (iv).\n(D) Abdominal muscles help us increase the strength of inspiration and expiration, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Contraction of the diaphragm increases the volume of the thoracic chamber in the dorso-ventral axis.\nStatement II: Contraction of the external intercostal muscles lifts the ribs and sternum, increasing thoracic volume in the dorso-ventral axis.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. When the diaphragm contracts, thoracic volume increases in the antero-posterior axis, not the dorso-ventral axis.\nStatement II is correct. The external intercostals lift the ribs and sternum, which enlarges the chamber dorso-ventrally.\nThe overall rise in thoracic volume lowers the intra-pulmonary pressure below atmospheric pressure, and air flows in.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (site) with List-II ($pO_2$ in mm Hg).\nList-I:\n(A) Atmospheric air\n(B) Alveoli\n(C) Deoxygenated blood\n(D) Oxygenated blood\nList-II:\n(i) 95\n(ii) 40\n(iii) 159\n(iv) 104","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Atmospheric air has a $pO_2$ of 159 mm Hg, so it matches (iii).\n(B) Alveoli have a $pO_2$ of 104 mm Hg, so it matches (iv).\n(C) Deoxygenated blood has a $pO_2$ of 40 mm Hg, so it matches (ii).\n(D) Oxygenated blood has a $pO_2$ of 95 mm Hg, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the binding of $CO_2$ to haemoglobin is correct?","image":"","options":["Binding is favoured when pCO2 is low and pO2 is high","pO2 has no effect on the binding of $CO_2$ to haemoglobin","Binding is favoured when pCO2 is high and pO2 is low, as in the tissues","$CO_2$ bound to haemoglobin is released mainly at the tissues"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: low pCO2 with high pO2 occurs in the alveoli, and these conditions favour dissociation of $CO_2$.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT calls pO2 a major factor affecting this binding.\n(3) Correct: high pCO2 with low pO2 occurs in the tissues and favours carbamino-haemoglobin formation.\n(4) Incorrect: bound $CO_2$ is released at the alveoli, not at the tissues.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The chemosensitive area situated adjacent to the respiratory rhythm centre is highly sensitive to:","image":"","options":["$CO_2$ and $H^+$ ions","$O_2$ and $H^+$ ions","$O_2$ and $CO_2$","$HCO_3^-$ and $O_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states the chemosensitive area is highly sensitive to $CO_2$ and hydrogen ions.\nA rise in these activates the area, which then signals the rhythm centre.\nAny option containing $O_2$ is wrong, because the role of oxygen in regulating the rhythm is quite insignificant.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In insects, atmospheric air is carried directly to the body tissues through which of the following?","image":"","options":["Vascularised gills","A network of tracheal tubes","Moist cuticle","Vascularised lung sacs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that insects have a network of tubes called tracheal tubes. These tubes carry atmospheric air within the body.\nGills are used by most aquatic arthropods and molluscs, not by insects.\nA moist cuticle is the respiratory surface of earthworms.\nLungs are vascularised bags used by terrestrial vertebrates.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"When carbon dioxide binds directly to haemoglobin in RBCs, the compound formed is called","image":"","options":["Oxyhaemoglobin","Carboxyhaemoglobin","Carbonic acid","Carbamino-haemoglobin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that $CO_2$ carried by haemoglobin forms carbamino-haemoglobin, which accounts for about 20-25 per cent of $CO_2$ transport.\nOxyhaemoglobin is the compound formed when haemoglobin binds $O_2$.\nCarbonic acid ($H_2CO_3$) forms from $CO_2$ and water, not from binding to haemoglobin.\nCarboxyhaemoglobin is formed with carbon monoxide, not with $CO_2$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Even after the most forceful expiration, the lungs of a healthy person are never completely emptied of air.\nReason (R): A residual volume of about 1100 to 1200 mL of air remains in the lungs after a forcible expiration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a forcible expiration expels only the ERV. Some air always stays in the lungs.\nR is true: the air that stays is the residual volume, which averages 1100 to 1200 mL.\nR directly explains A, because the residual volume is exactly why the lungs never empty fully.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A spirometer record of a person gives these values:\nTidal volume = 500 mL\nIRV = 3000 mL\nERV = 1000 mL\nResidual volume = 1200 mL\nMatch List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Inspiratory capacity\n(B) Functional residual capacity\n(C) Vital capacity\n(D) Total lung capacity\nList-II:\n(i) 2200 mL\n(ii) 3500 mL\n(iii) 4500 mL\n(iv) 5700 mL","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Inspiratory capacity = TV + IRV = 500 + 3000 = 3500 mL, so it matches (ii).\n(B) Functional residual capacity = ERV + RV = 1000 + 1200 = 2200 mL, so it matches (i).\n(C) Vital capacity = ERV + TV + IRV = 1000 + 500 + 3000 = 4500 mL, so it matches (iii).\n(D) Total lung capacity = VC + RV = 4500 + 1200 = 5700 mL, so it matches (iv).\nOption (2) swaps VC and TLC. TLC must be the larger value because it also includes the residual volume.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"At rest, a person's heart pumps 5 litres of oxygenated blood to the body tissues every minute. Assume normal physiological conditions and NCERT's value for oxygen delivered per 100 mL of oxygenated blood. How much oxygen is delivered to the tissues per minute?","image":"","options":["25 mL","200 mL","250 mL","500 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: blood flow = 5 L/min = 5000 mL/min.\nNCERT value: 100 mL of oxygenated blood delivers about 5 mL of O$_2$.\nFormula: O$_2$ delivered = (blood volume ÷ 100) × 5 mL.\nSubstituting: (5000 ÷ 100) × 5 = 50 × 5.\nSo the O$_2$ delivered = 250 mL per minute. The 200 mL option wrongly uses the CO$_2$ value of 4 mL.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Air moves into the lungs during inspiration when the intra-pulmonary pressure is:","image":"","options":["Higher than the atmospheric pressure","Less than the atmospheric pressure","Equal to the atmospheric pressure","Equal to the pressure in the pleural fluid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Air flows from a region of higher pressure to a region of lower pressure.\nInspiration happens when the pressure inside the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure. This is a negative pressure with respect to the atmosphere.\nIf the two pressures were equal, there would be no gradient and no airflow.\nIf the lung pressure were higher, air would flow out, which is expiration.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Larynx\n(b) Epiglottis\n(c) Trachea\n(d) Terminal bronchiole\nList-II:\n(i) Gives rise to thin, vascularised bag-like structures\n(ii) Divides at the level of the 5th thoracic vertebra\n(iii) Sound box\n(iv) Elastic cartilaginous flap over the glottis\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Larynx: the cartilaginous sound box, so (iii).\n(b) Epiglottis: the elastic cartilaginous flap that covers the glottis, so (iv).\n(c) Trachea: divides into primary bronchi at the 5th thoracic vertebra, so (ii).\n(d) Terminal bronchiole: gives rise to alveoli, the thin vascularised sacs, so (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The outer pleural membrane is in close contact with the lung surface, while the inner pleural membrane lines the thoracic wall.\nStatement II: The pleural fluid between the two pleural membranes reduces friction on the lung surface.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The positions are reversed: the outer pleural membrane touches the thoracic lining, and the inner pleural membrane touches the lung surface.\nStatement II is correct. Pleural fluid lies between the two layers and reduces friction on the lung surface.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The inner pleural membrane is in contact with the lung surface, while the outer pleural membrane is in close contact with the thoracic lining","Terminal bronchioles belong to the conducting part, while alveolar ducts belong to the exchange part","Diffusion of gases between blood and tissues is a step of respiration distinct from transport of gases by blood","The conducting part ends at the tertiary bronchi, and all branches beyond them take part in gas exchange"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT describes exactly this arrangement of the two pleural layers.\n(2) Correct: the conducting part ends at the terminal bronchioles, and the alveoli with their ducts form the exchange part.\n(3) Correct: these are listed as steps (iii) and (iv) of respiration.\n(4) Incorrect: bronchioles, including the terminal bronchioles, are still part of the conducting zone. Exchange occurs only in the alveoli and their ducts.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the human respiratory tract is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The pharynx opens through the larynx region into the trachea","The glottis is a thin elastic cartilaginous flap that covers the epiglottis during swallowing","Each terminal bronchiole gives rise to a number of thin, irregular-walled, vascularised alveoli","The external nostrils open out above the upper lips and lead to the nasal chamber through the nasal passage"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. The pharynx leads through the larynx into the trachea.\n(2) Incorrect. The roles are reversed: the epiglottis is the elastic cartilaginous flap, and it covers the glottis (the opening into the larynx).\n(3) Correct. Terminal bronchioles end in alveoli.\n(4) Correct. The nostrils lead to the nasal chamber through the nasal passage.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For inspiration to occur, the intra-pulmonary pressure must fall below the atmospheric pressure.\nStatement II: Contraction of the external intercostal muscles pulls the ribs and sternum downward, decreasing the dorso-ventral dimension of the thoracic chamber.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Air moves in only when there is a negative pressure in the lungs relative to the atmosphere.\nStatement II is incorrect. Contraction of the external intercostals lifts the ribs and sternum upward.\nThis increases, not decreases, the thoracic volume in the dorso-ventral axis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A person has difficulty in breathing, with a whistling (wheezing) sound during each breath. According to NCERT, this condition, asthma, results from","image":"","options":["damage to the walls of the alveoli","inflammation of the bronchi and bronchioles","proliferation of fibrous tissue in the lungs due to dust","reduction of pleural fluid between the pleural membranes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines asthma as difficulty in breathing, causing wheezing, due to inflammation of the bronchi and bronchioles.\nDamaged alveolar walls describe emphysema, not asthma.\nFibrosis from dust is an occupational respiratory disorder.\nThe pleural fluid option is not linked to asthma in the text.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Neural signals from the pneumotaxic centre can alter the respiratory rate.\nReason (R): Signals from the pneumotaxic centre can reduce the duration of inspiration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says the pneumotaxic centre moderates the rhythm centre and alters the respiratory rate.\nR is true. Its neural signal reduces the duration of inspiration.\nR explains A. Shortening each inspiration changes the length of each breathing cycle, and this alters the rate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following vertebrates respire through lungs?\nFrog, Lizard, Pigeon, Rohu, Whale, Crocodile, Shark","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Per NCERT, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals respire through lungs, while fishes use gills.\nFrog (amphibian): lungs.\nLizard and crocodile (reptiles): lungs.\nPigeon (bird): lungs.\nWhale (aquatic mammal): lungs.\nRohu and shark (fishes): gills.\nThat gives 5 lung-breathers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which structure of the human body has a portion that serves as a common passage for both food and air?","image":"","options":["Larynx","Trachea","Nasal chamber","Pharynx"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The nasal chamber opens into the pharynx.\nA portion of the pharynx is shared by food (going to the oesophagus) and air (going to the larynx).\nThe larynx and trachea carry only air.\nThe nasal chamber carries only air and does not receive food.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"What is the maximum number of oxygen molecules that a single haemoglobin molecule can carry?","image":"","options":["One","Two","Eight","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Haemoglobin is an iron-containing red pigment found in RBCs.\nIt binds O$_2$ reversibly to form oxyhaemoglobin.\nEach haemoglobin molecule can carry at most four O$_2$ molecules.\n'Eight' wrongly counts oxygen atoms instead of O$_2$ molecules.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Under normal physiological conditions, approximately how much $CO_2$ is delivered to the alveoli by every 100 mL of deoxygenated blood?","image":"","options":["4 mL","5 mL","20 mL","45 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that every 100 mL of deoxygenated blood delivers about 4 mL of $CO_2$ to the alveoli.\nThe value 5 mL is the amount of $O_2$ that 100 mL of oxygenated blood delivers to the tissues, so it should not be confused with the $CO_2$ figure.\nThe value 45 is the pCO2 of deoxygenated blood in mm Hg, not a volume.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At the alveoli, bicarbonate ions in the blood are converted back to $CO_2$ and $H_2O$.\nReason (R): pCO2 in the alveoli (about 40 mm Hg) is lower than in deoxygenated blood (about 45 mm Hg), so the carbonic anhydrase reaction proceeds in the reverse direction.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. At the alveoli, $HCO_3^- + H^+$ form $H_2CO_3$, which then gives $CO_2 + H_2O$.\nR is true. Alveolar pCO2 is 40 mm Hg and that of deoxygenated blood is 45 mm Hg.\nCarbonic anhydrase catalyses the reaction in both directions.\nThe low pCO2 at the alveoli drives the reaction backward, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the conducting part of the human respiratory system?","image":"","options":["Bringing inhaled air to body temperature","Clearing inhaled air of foreign particles","Humidifying the inhaled air","Diffusion of $O_2$ and $CO_2$ between blood and air"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, the conducting part transports atmospheric air to the alveoli.\nAlong the way it clears the air of foreign particles, humidifies it and brings it to body temperature.\nThe actual diffusion of $O_2$ and $CO_2$ between blood and air occurs only in the exchange part, i.e., the alveoli.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Apart from the chemosensitive area in the brain, receptors that recognise changes in $CO_2$ and $H^+$ concentration and signal the rhythm centre are associated with:","image":"","options":["Pulmonary vein and bronchioles","Alveoli and pleura","Trachea and larynx","Aortic arch and carotid artery"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that receptors associated with the aortic arch and carotid artery detect changes in $CO_2$ and $H^+$.\nThey send signals to the rhythm centre for remedial action.\nThe other options are respiratory or vascular structures that NCERT does not describe as having such receptors.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In human blood, the largest fraction of carbon dioxide is transported in which form?","image":"","options":["Dissolved in plasma","As carbamino-haemoglobin in RBCs","As bicarbonate ions","As undissociated carbonic acid in plasma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: about 70% of CO2 is carried as bicarbonate ($HCO_3^-$).\nCarbamino-haemoglobin carries about 20-25%. This is the most tempting option, but it is a smaller share.\nOnly about 7% of CO2 travels dissolved in plasma.\nCarbonic acid ($H_2CO_3$) is only a short-lived intermediate. It quickly dissociates into $HCO_3^-$ and $H^+$, so it is not a transport form.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about regulation of respiration in humans are correct?\n(i) The respiratory rhythm centre lies in the pons.\n(ii) The pneumotaxic centre can moderate the functions of the respiratory rhythm centre.\n(iii) An increase in $CO_2$ and $H^+$ activates the chemosensitive area.\n(iv) Receptors of the aortic arch and carotid artery send signals directly to the pneumotaxic centre.\n(v) The respiratory rhythm is regulated by the neural system.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the rhythm centre is in the medulla, not the pons.\n(ii) Correct: the pneumotaxic centre moderates the rhythm centre.\n(iii) Correct: a rise in $CO_2$ and $H^+$ activates the chemosensitive area.\n(iv) Incorrect: these receptors signal the rhythm centre, not the pneumotaxic centre.\n(v) Correct: NCERT says regulation is done by the neural system.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Oxygen plays a major role in regulating the human respiratory rhythm.\nReason (R): Receptors associated with the aortic arch and carotid artery can recognise changes in CO2 and H+ concentration and send signals to the rhythm centre.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. NCERT states that the role of oxygen in regulating respiratory rhythm is quite insignificant.\nR is true. Receptors in the aortic arch and carotid artery detect changes in CO2 and H+ and signal the respiratory rhythm centre to take remedial action.\nRegulation therefore depends mainly on CO2 and H+, not on O2.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following belongs to the respiratory or exchange part of the human respiratory system?","image":"","options":["Terminal bronchioles","Tertiary bronchi","Alveolar ducts","Trachea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The conducting part runs from the external nostrils up to the terminal bronchioles.\nThe alveoli and their ducts form the respiratory or exchange part.\nTerminal bronchioles are the most tempting distractor, but they are the last segment of the conducting part.\nThe trachea and tertiary bronchi are also conducting structures.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (respiratory volume) with List-II (average value in a healthy adult):\nList-I\n(a) Tidal volume\n(b) Inspiratory reserve volume\n(c) Expiratory reserve volume\n(d) Residual volume\nList-II\n(i) 1100 to 1200 mL\n(ii) About 500 mL\n(iii) 1000 to 1100 mL\n(iv) 2500 to 3000 mL","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Tidal volume is about 500 mL, so (ii).\n(b) IRV is 2500 to 3000 mL, so (iv).\n(c) ERV is 1000 to 1100 mL, so (iii).\n(d) RV is 1100 to 1200 mL, so (i).\nERV and RV have close values. Remember that RV is slightly larger.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Nearly 70 per cent of carbon dioxide is transported in blood as bicarbonate.\nStatement II: The enzyme carbonic anhydrase is present only in RBCs and is completely absent from plasma.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. About 70% of CO2 travels as bicarbonate, 20–25% as carbamino-haemoglobin and about 7% dissolved in plasma.\nStatement II is incorrect. RBCs contain a very high concentration of carbonic anhydrase, but minute quantities are also present in plasma.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Contraction of the diaphragm increases the volume of the thoracic chamber along which axis?","image":"","options":["Dorso-ventral axis","Lateral axis only","Antero-posterior axis","No axis, because the diaphragm acts only during expiration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that inspiration begins with contraction of the dome-shaped diaphragm.\nThis contraction enlarges the thoracic chamber in the antero-posterior axis.\nThe dorso-ventral increase is a tempting choice, but it comes from the external intercostals lifting the ribs and sternum.\nThe diaphragm relaxes during expiration, so it is not an expiratory muscle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the mechanism of breathing is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Expiration occurs when intra-pulmonary pressure is higher than atmospheric pressure","Contraction of external intercostal muscles lifts the ribs and sternum, increasing thoracic volume in the antero-posterior axis","An increase in thoracic volume causes a similar increase in pulmonary volume","The diaphragm and intercostal muscles help generate pressure gradients between the lungs and the atmosphere"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: air leaves the lungs when the pressure inside them exceeds atmospheric pressure.\n(2) Incorrect: lifting the ribs and sternum enlarges the thorax in the dorso-ventral axis. The antero-posterior increase is caused by the diaphragm.\n(3) Correct: the thorax and lungs are arranged so that a change in thoracic volume is reflected in pulmonary volume.\n(4) Correct: these muscles create the pressure gradients that drive breathing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Sponges, coelenterates and flatworms exchange $O_2$ and $CO_2$ with their surroundings mainly by:","image":"","options":["Tracheal tubes that carry atmospheric air inside the body","Simple diffusion over the entire body surface","Vascularised gills","Vascularised lungs aided by a moist skin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT groups sponges, coelenterates and flatworms as lower invertebrates.\nThey have no special respiratory organ. Gases are exchanged by simple diffusion over the whole body surface.\nTracheal tubes belong to insects. Gills belong to aquatic arthropods, molluscs and fishes. Lungs belong to terrestrial forms.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Asthma involves inflammation of the bronchi and bronchioles.\n(ii) Emphysema reduces the surface available for gas exchange.\n(iii) Occupational respiratory disorders are mainly caused by cigarette smoking.\n(iv) Prolonged inhalation of industrial dust can lead to fibrosis.\n(v) Emphysema is an acute disorder that heals within a few days.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the NCERT description of asthma.\n(ii) Correct: damaged alveolar walls reduce the respiratory surface.\n(iii) Incorrect: occupational disorders are caused by dust from grinding and stone-breaking; smoking is linked to emphysema.\n(iv) Correct: long exposure to dust causes inflammation leading to fibrosis.\n(v) Incorrect: NCERT calls emphysema a chronic disorder.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (side of the thoracic chamber) with List-II (structure forming it).\nList-I:\n(a) Dorsal side\n(b) Ventral side\n(c) Lateral sides\n(d) Lower side\nList-II:\n(i) Ribs\n(ii) Diaphragm\n(iii) Vertebral column\n(iv) Sternum","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The dorsal side is formed by the vertebral column, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) The ventral side is formed by the sternum, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) The lateral sides are formed by the ribs, so (c)-(i).\n(d) The lower side is formed by the dome-shaped diaphragm, so (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose experimental stimulation strengthens the neural signals from the pneumotaxic centre. Based on the NCERT description of this centre, the most likely immediate effect on breathing would be:","image":"","options":["Shorter inspiration with an increase in respiratory rate","Longer inspiration with a decrease in respiratory rate","No change in breathing, since only $O_2$ levels control the rhythm centre","Complete stoppage of the respiratory rhythm centre's activity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT says pneumotaxic signals reduce the duration of inspiration and thereby alter the respiratory rate.\nStronger signals would shorten each inspiration further.\nShorter breathing cycles mean more breaths per minute, so the rate rises.\nOption 2 is the opposite effect.\nOption 3 is wrong because the role of $O_2$ is insignificant.\nOption 4 is wrong because the centre only moderates the rhythm centre and does not abolish its activity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Using the NCERT values, how does blood leaving the alveolar capillaries (oxygenated blood) compare with alveolar air?","image":"","options":["$pO_2$ equal; $pCO_2$ lower in blood","$pO_2$ lower in blood; $pCO_2$ equal","$pO_2$ higher in blood; $pCO_2$ higher in blood","$pO_2$ lower in blood; $pCO_2$ higher in blood"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Oxygenated blood has a $pO_2$ of 95 mm Hg, while alveolar air has 104 mm Hg, so $pO_2$ is lower in the blood.\nOxygenated blood has a $pCO_2$ of 40 mm Hg, the same as alveolar air at 40 mm Hg, so $pCO_2$ is equal.\nOption 4 is tempting, but a $pCO_2$ of 45 mm Hg belongs to deoxygenated blood, not oxygenated blood.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Contraction of diaphragm\n(B) Contraction of external intercostal muscles\n(C) Relaxation of diaphragm and intercostals\n(D) Additional abdominal muscles\nList-II\n(i) Increase in thoracic volume in dorso-ventral axis\n(ii) Increase in thoracic volume in antero-posterior axis\n(iii) Increase the strength of inspiration and expiration\n(iv) Decrease in thoracic volume leading to expiration","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(A) Diaphragm contraction enlarges the thorax in the antero-posterior axis, so A matches (ii).\n(B) External intercostal contraction lifts the ribs and sternum, enlarging the thorax in the dorso-ventral axis, so B matches (i).\n(C) Relaxation of these muscles reduces thoracic volume, which causes expiration, so C matches (iv).\n(D) Abdominal muscles can increase the strength of both inspiration and expiration, so D matches (iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy adult has a tidal volume of 500 mL, an inspiratory reserve volume of 2500 mL, an expiratory reserve volume of 1000 mL and a residual volume of 1100 mL. What is the vital capacity of this person?","image":"","options":["3000 mL","4000 mL","3500 mL","5100 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: TV = 500 mL, IRV = 2500 mL, ERV = 1000 mL, RV = 1100 mL.\nFormula: VC = ERV + TV + IRV. Residual volume is not included in VC.\nSubstituting: VC = 1000 + 500 + 2500.\nSo VC = 4000 mL.\nThe value 5100 mL wrongly adds RV, which gives the total lung capacity instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about respiratory disorders is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Emphysema leads to a decrease in the respiratory surface.","Long exposure to dust in stone-breaking industries can cause serious lung damage.","Wheezing is a characteristic feature of asthma.","Asthma is a chronic disorder in which the alveolar walls are damaged, mainly due to smoking."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the damaged alveolar walls in emphysema decrease the respiratory surface.\n(2) Correct: dust exposure causes inflammation and fibrosis, which seriously damages the lungs.\n(3) Correct: asthma causes wheezing.\n(4) Incorrect: damaged alveolar walls and smoking describe emphysema; asthma is inflammation of the bronchi and bronchioles.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Vital capacity of the lungs is best defined as the:","image":"","options":["volume of air remaining in the lungs after a normal expiration","total volume of air in the lungs at the end of a forced inspiration","maximum volume of air a person can breathe out after a forced inspiration","volume of air inspired or expired during normal quiet breathing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines vital capacity (VC) as the maximum volume of air a person can breathe in after a forced expiration. Equivalently, it is the maximum volume a person can breathe out after a forced inspiration.\nVC = ERV + TV + IRV.\nOption (2) describes total lung capacity, which also includes RV. Option (1) is FRC, and option (4) is tidal volume.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A rise in CO$_2$ and H$^+$ concentration in the blood leads to adjustment of the respiratory process so that these substances are eliminated.\nReason (R): Neural signals from the pneumotaxic centre in the pons can reduce the duration of inspiration and thereby alter the respiratory rate.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. A rise in CO$_2$ and H$^+$ activates the chemosensitive area next to the respiratory rhythm centre in the medulla. Receptors on the aortic arch and carotid artery also detect these changes. Both send signals to the rhythm centre to adjust breathing.\nR is true. The pneumotaxic centre in the pons moderates the rhythm centre by shortening inspiration.\nHowever, R describes the pneumotaxic centre, not the chemoreceptor response to CO$_2$ and H$^+$, so it does not explain A. Note also that O$_2$ plays an insignificant role in regulating respiratory rhythm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (site) with List-II (pO$_2$, pCO$_2$ in mm Hg).\nList-I:\n(A) Atmospheric air\n(B) Alveoli\n(C) Deoxygenated blood\n(D) Oxygenated blood\nList-II:\n(i) 95, 40\n(ii) 159, 0.3\n(iii) 40, 45\n(iv) 104, 40\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Atmospheric air has the highest pO$_2$ (159) and almost no CO$_2$ (0.3), so A-(ii).\n(B) Alveolar air has pO$_2$ 104 and pCO$_2$ 40, so B-(iv).\n(C) Deoxygenated blood has the lowest pO$_2$ (40) and the highest pCO$_2$ (45), the same values as tissues, so C-(iii).\n(D) Oxygenated blood has pO$_2$ 95 and pCO$_2$ 40, so D-(i).\nThese values show O$_2$ moving down its gradient from alveoli to blood to tissues, while CO$_2$ moves the opposite way.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy student sitting quietly in an exam hall breathes 14 times per minute with a tidal volume of 500 mL. What total volume of air does she inspire during a 30-minute period?","image":"","options":["7 L","21 L","210 L","420 L"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: TV = 500 mL, rate = 14 breaths/min, time = 30 min.\nFormula: Volume = TV × rate × time.\nSubstituting: 500 × 14 = 7000 mL/min. Then 7000 × 30 = 210,000 mL.\nConverting: 210,000 mL = 210 L.\nThe 7 L option is only the one-minute value.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A penetrating chest injury makes the right side of a person's thoracic chamber open to the outside air, so it is no longer air-tight. The person's diaphragm and intercostal muscles still contract normally. What is the most likely effect on the right lung during inspiration?","image":"","options":["It inflates more than normal because outside air enters the thoracic cavity directly","It fails to expand properly because the enlargement of the thoracic cavity is no longer transmitted to the pulmonary cavity, so intra-pulmonary pressure does not fall below atmospheric","It inflates normally because diaphragm contraction directly increases pulmonary volume","It expands normally, but gas exchange stops because the alveolar membrane thickens"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"We cannot change pulmonary volume directly.\nThe lungs sit in an air-tight thoracic chamber, so any change in thoracic volume is reflected in the lung (pulmonary) cavity.\nIf the chamber is open to the outside, enlarging the thorax no longer pulls the lung outward.\nPulmonary volume therefore does not increase, and intra-pulmonary pressure does not drop below atmospheric. Little air enters that lung.\nOption (3) is wrong because the diaphragm acts on the thorax, not directly on the lung.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about the regulation of respiration in humans:\n(i) A neural signal from the pneumotaxic centre can reduce the duration of inspiration and thereby alter the respiratory rate.\n(ii) The chemosensitive area adjacent to the rhythm centre is highly sensitive to $CO_2$ and $H^+$ ions.\n(iii) Receptors in the aortic arch and carotid artery can recognise changes in $CO_2$ and $H^+$ concentration and signal the rhythm centre.\n(iv) A fall in blood $O_2$ is the most significant chemical stimulus regulating the respiratory rhythm.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the pneumotaxic centre in the pons shortens the duration of inspiration, which alters the breathing rate.\n(ii) Correct: the chemosensitive area next to the rhythm centre responds to rises in $CO_2$ and $H^+$.\n(iii) Correct: receptors in the aortic arch and carotid artery detect $CO_2$ and $H^+$ changes and signal the rhythm centre.\n(iv) Incorrect: NCERT states that the role of oxygen in regulating the respiratory rhythm is quite insignificant.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is described in NCERT as one of the major causes of emphysema?","image":"","options":["Inhaling dust during stone-breaking","Infection of the larynx","Cigarette smoking","Excess production of pleural fluid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Emphysema is a chronic disorder in which the alveolar walls are damaged, which reduces the respiratory surface.\nNCERT names cigarette smoking as one of its major causes.\nDust from grinding or stone-breaking is the cause NCERT gives for occupational respiratory disorders, not emphysema.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Air entering through the external nostrils passes through the following structures before reaching the alveoli:\n(a) Nasal chamber (b) Larynx (c) Tertiary bronchi (d) Pharynx (e) Terminal bronchioles (f) Primary bronchi (g) Trachea (h) Nasal passage\nWhich option gives the correct sequence?","image":"","options":["(a) → (h) → (d) → (b) → (g) → (f) → (c) → (e)","(h) → (a) → (b) → (d) → (g) → (f) → (c) → (e)","(h) → (a) → (d) → (b) → (g) → (f) → (c) → (e)","(h) → (a) → (d) → (g) → (b) → (f) → (c) → (e)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The external nostrils lead through the nasal passage (h) into the nasal chamber (a).\nThe nasal chamber opens into the pharynx (d), which opens through the larynx (b) into the trachea (g).\nThe trachea divides into the primary bronchi (f). These branch into secondary and then tertiary bronchi (c), then bronchioles, ending in terminal bronchioles (e) that give rise to alveoli.\nOption (1) puts the chamber before the passage, (2) puts the larynx before the pharynx, and (4) puts the trachea before the larynx.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In humans, the specialised centre that is primarily responsible for regulating the respiratory rhythm is located in which region of the brain?","image":"","options":["Pons","Medulla","Cerebellum","Hypothalamus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the respiratory rhythm centre lies in the medulla region of the brain.\nThis centre is primarily responsible for regulating breathing.\nThe pons is the tempting wrong option. It houses the pneumotaxic centre, which only moderates the rhythm centre.\nThe cerebellum and hypothalamus have no role in this NCERT account.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly shows the path of inhaled air in humans?","image":"","options":["External nostrils → nasal chamber → larynx → pharynx → trachea → bronchi → alveoli","External nostrils → nasal passage → nasal chamber → pharynx → larynx → trachea → primary bronchi → secondary bronchi → tertiary bronchi → bronchioles → terminal bronchioles → alveoli","External nostrils → nasal chamber → pharynx → trachea → larynx → primary bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli","External nostrils → nasal passage → nasal chamber → pharynx → larynx → trachea → primary bronchi → tertiary bronchi → secondary bronchi → terminal bronchioles → alveoli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The nostrils lead through the nasal passage to the nasal chamber, which opens into the pharynx.\nThe pharynx opens through the larynx into the trachea.\nThe trachea divides into primary bronchi, which branch into secondary and then tertiary bronchi, then bronchioles and terminal bronchioles, ending in alveoli.\nOption 1 puts the larynx before the pharynx. Option 3 puts the trachea before the larynx. Option 4 swaps the tertiary and secondary bronchi.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Frogs can exchange gases through their skin, a process called cutaneous respiration.\nReason (R): Frogs lack lungs, so the skin is their only respiratory surface.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that frogs can respire through their moist skin, which is cutaneous respiration.\nR is false. Amphibians, including frogs, respire through lungs. NCERT says frogs use the skin 'also', meaning in addition to their lungs.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Asthma\nB. Emphysema\nC. Occupational respiratory disorder\nD. Fibrosis\nList-II\n(i) Proliferation of fibrous tissues\n(ii) Inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles\n(iii) Damaged alveolar walls\n(iv) Dust from grinding and stone-breaking industries\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A–(ii): Asthma is inflammation of the bronchi and bronchioles.\nB–(iii): In emphysema, the alveolar walls are damaged.\nC–(iv): Occupational disorders arise from dust produced in grinding and stone-breaking industries.\nD–(i): Fibrosis is the proliferation of fibrous tissues.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Workers in grinding and stone-breaking industries are advised to wear protective masks.\nReason (R): In such industries, so much dust is produced that the body's defence mechanism cannot fully cope with it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that workers in these industries should wear protective masks.\nR is true: the dust load is so high that the body's defence mechanism cannot fully cope with it.\nWithout protection, long exposure causes inflammation and then fibrosis, which seriously damages the lungs.\nMasks are recommended precisely because the natural defences are overwhelmed, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Emphysema is a chronic disorder in which alveolar walls are damaged, so the respiratory surface is decreased.\nStatement II: Asthma is caused by fibrosis of lung tissue after long exposure to dust in stone-breaking industries.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In emphysema the alveolar walls are damaged and the respiratory surface shrinks. Cigarette smoking is a major cause.\nStatement II is incorrect. Asthma is difficulty in breathing, with wheezing, caused by inflammation of the bronchi and bronchioles. Fibrosis from long dust exposure describes occupational respiratory disorders, not asthma.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Neural signals from the pneumotaxic centre in the pons can reduce the duration of inspiration and thereby alter the respiratory rate","Under normal physiological conditions, every 100 mL of oxygenated blood delivers about 5 mL of O2 to the tissues","The trachea divides into right and left primary bronchi at the level of the 5th thoracic vertebra","Binding of CO2 to haemoglobin as carbamino-haemoglobin is favoured where pCO2 is low and pO2 is high"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the pneumotaxic centre moderates the rhythm centre by shortening inspiration.\n(2) Correct: 100 mL of oxygenated blood delivers about 5 mL of O2 to the tissues.\n(3) Correct: the trachea bifurcates at the 5th thoracic vertebra.\n(4) Incorrect: CO2 binds haemoglobin when pCO2 is high and pO2 is low, as in the tissues. Where pCO2 is low and pO2 is high, as in the alveoli, CO2 dissociates from carbamino-haemoglobin.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Use the NCERT values of partial pressures (mm Hg) given below:\nO2: atmospheric air 159, alveoli 104, deoxygenated blood 40, oxygenated blood 95, tissues 40\nCO2: atmospheric air 0.3, alveoli 40, deoxygenated blood 45, oxygenated blood 40, tissues 45\nHow many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The pO2 of deoxygenated blood equals the pO2 of tissues.\n(ii) The pCO2 of alveolar air equals the pCO2 of oxygenated blood.\n(iii) The pCO2 of atmospheric air is higher than that of alveolar air.\n(iv) The pO2 gradient between alveoli and deoxygenated blood is 64 mm Hg.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: both values are 40 mm Hg.\n(ii) Correct: both values are 40 mm Hg.\n(iii) Incorrect: atmospheric pCO2 is 0.3 mm Hg, which is far lower than the alveolar value of 40 mm Hg.\n(iv) Correct: 104 − 40 = 64 mm Hg.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Spirometry of a patient gives the following values: total lung capacity = 5800 mL, inspiratory capacity = 3500 mL, residual volume = 1200 mL and tidal volume = 500 mL. What is the expiratory capacity of this patient?","image":"","options":["1600 mL","2300 mL","1100 mL","4000 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: TLC = 5800 mL, IC = 3500 mL, RV = 1200 mL, TV = 500 mL.\nStep 1: TLC = IRV + TV + ERV + RV, which can be written as IC + ERV + RV.\nSo ERV = 5800 − 3500 − 1200 = 1100 mL.\nStep 2: Expiratory capacity = TV + ERV = 500 + 1100 = 1600 mL.\nThe value 2300 mL is the FRC (ERV + RV), and 1100 mL is ERV alone.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following conditions favour the dissociation of oxygen from oxyhaemoglobin?\nHigh pCO$_2$; Low H$^+$ concentration; Higher temperature; Low pO$_2$; High pO$_2$; Lower temperature","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says tissue conditions favour dissociation: low pO$_2$, high pCO$_2$, high H$^+$ and higher temperature.\nHigh pCO$_2$: favours dissociation.\nLow H$^+$: no, it favours binding.\nHigher temperature: favours dissociation.\nLow pO$_2$: favours dissociation.\nHigh pO$_2$ and lower temperature: no, both favour binding.\nSo 3 conditions favour dissociation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The diffusion membrane between alveolar air and blood is made of three major layers. Which of the following is NOT one of these layers?","image":"","options":["Thin squamous epithelium of the alveolus","Endothelium of the alveolar capillary","Basement substance lying between the epithelium and the endothelium","Ciliated columnar epithelium of the terminal bronchiole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists three layers in the diffusion membrane:\n(i) the squamous epithelium of the alveolus;\n(ii) the endothelium of the alveolar capillary;\n(iii) the basement substance between them, made of the two basement membranes.\nTerminal bronchioles belong to the conducting part of the respiratory system, where no gas exchange takes place. So their lining is not part of the diffusion membrane.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose 5 litres of deoxygenated blood pass through the lungs of a resting person in one minute. Using the NCERT value for $CO_2$ delivery under normal conditions, about how much $CO_2$ is delivered to the alveoli in that minute?","image":"","options":["20 mL","200 mL","250 mL","2000 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 100 mL of deoxygenated blood delivers about 4 mL of $CO_2$. The blood volume is 5 L = 5000 mL.\nCalculation: $CO_2$ delivered = (5000/100) × 4 mL.\nThis gives 50 × 4 = 200 mL.\n250 mL would result from wrongly using the oxygen figure of 5 mL per 100 mL.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following pairs of animal and its mode of gas exchange are correctly matched?\n(i) Hydra – diffusion over entire body surface\n(ii) Spongilla – gills\n(iii) Grasshopper – tracheal tubes\n(iv) Unio (freshwater mussel) – gills\n(v) Earthworm – lungs\n(vi) Liver fluke – diffusion over body surface","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Hydra is a coelenterate and exchanges gases over its body surface.\n(ii) Incorrect: Spongilla is a sponge and relies on simple diffusion, not gills.\n(iii) Correct: Grasshopper is an insect with tracheal tubes.\n(iv) Correct: Unio is an aquatic mollusc that uses gills.\n(v) Incorrect: Earthworm uses its moist cuticle, not lungs.\n(vi) Correct: Liver fluke is a flatworm that relies on body-surface diffusion.\nFour pairs are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The sum of inspiratory capacity and expiratory reserve volume equals vital capacity.\nStatement II: The sum of expiratory capacity and inspiratory reserve volume also equals vital capacity.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: IC + ERV = (TV + IRV) + ERV. This equals VC, so it is correct.\nStatement II: EC + IRV = (TV + ERV) + IRV. This also equals VC, so it is correct.\nBoth expressions add up the same three volumes: TV, IRV and ERV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following conditions favour the formation of oxyhaemoglobin?\nHigh pO$_2$\nLow pCO$_2$\nHigh H$^+$ concentration\nHigher temperature\nLower H$^+$ concentration\nLower temperature","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Two"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The alveoli show the conditions that favour formation of oxyhaemoglobin: high pO$_2$, low pCO$_2$, lesser H$^+$ and lower temperature.\nFavourable: high pO$_2$, low pCO$_2$, lower H$^+$, lower temperature. That makes 4.\nHigh H$^+$ and higher temperature are tissue conditions. They favour dissociation of oxyhaemoglobin, not its formation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The largest fraction of carbon dioxide in human blood is transported:","image":"","options":["As carbamino-haemoglobin in RBCs","As bicarbonate","In dissolved state in plasma","Bound to the iron of haemoglobin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"About 70% of $CO_2$ is carried as bicarbonate, which forms with the help of carbonic anhydrase.\nAbout 20-25% travels as carbamino-haemoglobin, which is the tempting distractor.\nOnly about 7% is carried dissolved in plasma.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The trachea is a straight tube that extends up to the mid-thoracic cavity.\nStatement II: Terminal bronchioles are supported by incomplete cartilaginous rings, which keep them from collapsing.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The trachea runs straight down to the mid-thoracic cavity and divides there.\nStatement II is incorrect. Incomplete cartilaginous rings support only the trachea, the bronchi and the initial bronchioles.\nTerminal bronchioles are very thin and have no cartilage.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Total lung capacity differs from vital capacity by which respiratory volume?","image":"","options":["Tidal volume","Expiratory reserve volume","Inspiratory reserve volume","Residual volume"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"TLC = RV + ERV + TV + IRV.\nVC = ERV + TV + IRV.\nSo TLC − VC = RV, the air that remains in the lungs even after a forcible expiration.\nERV, TV and IRV are part of both capacities, so none of them can be the difference.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Under normal physiological conditions, approximately how much oxygen does every 100 mL of oxygenated blood deliver to the tissues?","image":"","options":["4 mL","20 mL","5 mL","1 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that every 100 mL of oxygenated blood delivers about 5 mL of O$_2$ to the tissues under normal conditions.\nThe value 4 mL is the amount of CO$_2$ that 100 mL of deoxygenated blood delivers to the alveoli. It is a common mix-up.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At the alveolar membrane, the partial pressure difference for $CO_2$ is much smaller than that for $O_2$, yet $CO_2$ diffuses efficiently.\nReason (R): The solubility of $CO_2$ is 20-25 times higher than that of $O_2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The $pO_2$ gradient is 104 − 40 = 64 mm Hg (alveoli to deoxygenated blood), while the $pCO_2$ gradient is only 45 − 40 = 5 mm Hg (blood to alveoli). $CO_2$ still diffuses efficiently.\nR is true. $CO_2$ is 20-25 times more soluble than $O_2$.\nR explains A, because higher solubility means far more $CO_2$ diffuses per unit difference in partial pressure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy adult has the following lung volumes: tidal volume = 500 mL, IRV = 2800 mL, ERV = 1100 mL and residual volume = 1200 mL. What volume of air is present in this person's lungs at the end of a normal inspiration?","image":"","options":["2300 mL","2800 mL","4400 mL","5600 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: TV = 500 mL, IRV = 2800 mL, ERV = 1100 mL, RV = 1200 mL.\nAfter a normal expiration, the lungs hold the FRC, which is ERV + RV.\nA normal inspiration then adds one tidal volume, so the volume = ERV + RV + TV.\nSubstituting: 1100 + 1200 + 500 = 2800 mL.\nThe distractors are FRC (2300 mL), vital capacity (4400 mL) and total lung capacity (5600 mL).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following combinations of respiratory volumes gives the functional residual capacity (FRC)?","image":"","options":["Tidal volume + Inspiratory reserve volume","Expiratory reserve volume + Residual volume","Tidal volume + Expiratory reserve volume","Inspiratory reserve volume + Residual volume"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"FRC is the volume of air that stays in the lungs after a normal expiration.\nAfter a normal expiration, the lungs still hold the air you could forcibly expire (ERV). They also hold the air that can never be expelled (RV).\nSo FRC = ERV + RV.\nTV + IRV is the inspiratory capacity, and TV + ERV is the expiratory capacity. Neither describes air left in the lungs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A graph is drawn with the partial pressure of oxygen (mm Hg) on the x-axis and the percentage saturation of haemoglobin with oxygen on the y-axis. What is the shape of the curve obtained?","image":"","options":["Sigmoid (S-shaped)","Straight line passing through the origin","Hyperbolic, rising steeply from the start","Bell-shaped, with a peak at intermediate pO$_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Plotting % saturation of Hb against pO$_2$ gives the oxygen dissociation curve.\nNCERT states that this curve is sigmoid, i.e., S-shaped.\nIt is not linear, and it does not fall after a peak as a bell-shaped curve would.\nThe curve is used to study how pCO$_2$, H$^+$ and other factors affect O$_2$ binding.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The enzyme carbonic anhydrase, which speeds up the reaction $CO_2 + H_2O \\rightleftharpoons H_2CO_3$, is present in a very high concentration in:","image":"","options":["Blood plasma","Red blood cells","Alveolar squamous epithelium","White blood cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that RBCs contain a very high concentration of carbonic anhydrase.\nPlasma has only minute quantities of the enzyme. So plasma is a tempting but wrong choice: it has the enzyme, just not in high concentration.\nThe alveolar epithelium and WBCs are not described as sites of this enzyme.\nThis is why most bicarbonate is formed inside RBCs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"For a healthy person, the total lung capacity is 5800 mL, the functional residual capacity is 2300 mL, the expiratory capacity is 1600 mL and the tidal volume is 500 mL. What is the vital capacity?","image":"","options":["3500 mL","4100 mL","4600 mL","5300 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: TLC = 5800 mL, FRC = 2300 mL, EC = 1600 mL, TV = 500 mL.\nStep 1: ERV = EC − TV = 1600 − 500 = 1100 mL.\nStep 2: RV = FRC − ERV = 2300 − 1100 = 1200 mL.\nStep 3: VC = TLC − RV = 5800 − 1200 = 4600 mL.\nCheck: IRV = TLC − FRC − TV = 3000 mL, and VC = 3000 + 500 + 1100 = 4600 mL.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Under normal physiological conditions, what percentage of oxygen is carried in the blood in a dissolved state through the plasma?","image":"","options":["About 97 per cent","About 3 per cent","About 7 per cent","About 20-25 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"About 97% of O$_2$ is carried by RBCs, bound to haemoglobin.\nThe remaining 3% is carried dissolved in the plasma.\n7% is the share of CO$_2$ (not O$_2$) carried dissolved in plasma, so that option is a trap.\n20-25% is the share of CO$_2$ carried as carbamino-haemoglobin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The functional residual capacity (FRC) of human lungs is the sum of which two respiratory volumes?","image":"","options":["Tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume","Expiratory reserve volume and residual volume","Tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume","Inspiratory reserve volume and residual volume"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"FRC is the volume of air that stays in the lungs after a normal expiration.\nAfter a normal expiration, the lungs still hold the air you could forcibly breathe out (ERV) plus the air that can never be breathed out (RV).\nSo FRC = ERV + RV.\nOption (3), TV + ERV, is the expiratory capacity. Option (1), TV + IRV, is the inspiratory capacity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In a person, tidal volume = 500 mL, IRV = 2500 mL, ERV = 1000 mL and RV = 1200 mL. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Inspiratory capacity = 3000 mL\n(ii) Functional residual capacity = 2200 mL\n(iii) Vital capacity = 4000 mL\n(iv) Expiratory capacity = 1700 mL\n(v) Total lung capacity = 5200 mL","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: IC = TV + IRV = 500 + 2500 = 3000 mL.\n(ii) Correct: FRC = ERV + RV = 1000 + 1200 = 2200 mL.\n(iii) Correct: VC = ERV + TV + IRV = 4000 mL.\n(iv) Incorrect: EC = TV + ERV = 1500 mL. The value 1700 mL wrongly adds RV to TV.\n(v) Correct: TLC = VC + RV = 4000 + 1200 = 5200 mL.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which instrument is used to estimate the volume of air involved in breathing movements and helps in the clinical assessment of pulmonary functions?","image":"","options":["Sphygmomanometer","Spirometer","Stethoscope","Electrocardiograph"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A spirometer measures the volumes of air inspired and expired. These values are used clinically to assess how well the lungs are working.\nA sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure.\nA stethoscope is used to listen to body sounds.\nAn electrocardiograph records the electrical activity of the heart.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Mode of transport)\n(A) $CO_2$ as bicarbonate\n(B) $CO_2$ as carbamino-haemoglobin\n(C) $CO_2$ dissolved in plasma\n(D) $O_2$ dissolved in plasma\nList-II (Approximate share)\n(I) 3 per cent\n(II) 7 per cent\n(III) 20-25 per cent\n(IV) 70 per cent","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Bicarbonate carries about 70 per cent of $CO_2$, so A-IV.\n(B) Carbamino-haemoglobin carries about 20-25 per cent of $CO_2$, so B-III.\n(C) About 7 per cent of $CO_2$ is dissolved in plasma, so C-II.\n(D) About 3 per cent of $O_2$ is carried dissolved in plasma, so D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although the pCO$_2$ difference between deoxygenated blood and alveolar air is only about 5 mm Hg, CO$_2$ is efficiently removed at the alveoli.\nReason (R): The solubility of CO$_2$ is 20-25 times higher than that of O$_2$, so more CO$_2$ diffuses per unit difference in partial pressure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. pCO$_2$ is 45 mm Hg in deoxygenated blood and 40 mm Hg in the alveoli, a gradient of only about 5 mm Hg. CO$_2$ still diffuses out effectively.\nR is true. CO$_2$ is 20-25 times more soluble than O$_2$, so much more of it crosses the membrane per unit partial-pressure difference.\nThis high solubility is exactly why a small gradient is enough, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Terminal bronchioles are the last structures of the conducting part of the respiratory system.\n(ii) The conducting part humidifies the inspired air and brings it to body temperature.\n(iii) The outer pleural membrane is in close contact with the lung surface.\n(iv) Diffusion of $O_2$ and $CO_2$ between blood and tissues comes after transport of gases by blood and before utilisation of $O_2$ by cells.\n(v) Initial bronchioles lack cartilaginous rings.\n(vi) During pulmonary ventilation, $CO_2$-rich alveolar air is released out.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the conducting part extends from the external nostrils to the terminal bronchioles.\n(ii) Correct: the conducting part also clears foreign particles from the air.\n(iii) Incorrect: the outer pleura touches the thoracic lining, while the inner pleura touches the lung surface.\n(iv) Correct: blood–tissue diffusion is step (iv) of respiration, between transport (iii) and cellular utilisation (v).\n(v) Incorrect: the initial bronchioles are supported by incomplete cartilaginous rings.\n(vi) Correct: breathing draws in atmospheric air and releases $CO_2$-rich alveolar air.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Using the average values given in NCERT, how many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) At the lowest normal breathing rate, a tidal volume of 500 mL gives the lower limit of the per-minute figure of 6000 to 8000 mL.\n(ii) Inspiratory reserve volume is larger than expiratory reserve volume.\n(iii) The average residual volume is slightly greater than the average expiratory reserve volume.\n(iv) Residual volume can be expelled from the lungs by a forcible expiration.\n(v) Tidal volume is smaller than each of IRV, ERV and RV.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: 500 mL × 12 breaths/min = 6000 mL/min.\n(ii) Correct: IRV is 2500 to 3000 mL, while ERV is 1000 to 1100 mL.\n(iii) Correct: RV is 1100 to 1200 mL, while ERV is 1000 to 1100 mL.\n(iv) Incorrect: RV remains in the lungs even after a forcible expiration.\n(v) Correct: 500 mL is less than all of the other three volumes.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The pneumotaxic centre, which can moderate the functions of the respiratory rhythm centre, is present in the:","image":"","options":["Medulla oblongata","Cerebrum","Pons","Spinal cord"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT places the pneumotaxic centre in the pons region of the brain.\nIt moderates the respiratory rhythm centre, which lies in the medulla.\nMedulla is the tempting option, but it holds the rhythm centre and the chemosensitive area, not the pneumotaxic centre.\nThe cerebrum and spinal cord are not described as respiratory centres.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In a healthy adult, the volume of air that still remains in the lungs even after a forcible expiration averages about:","image":"","options":["500 mL","1000 mL to 1100 mL","1100 mL to 1200 mL","2500 mL to 3000 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The air left in the lungs after a forcible expiration is the residual volume (RV). NCERT gives its average as 1100 mL to 1200 mL.\nThe most tempting wrong option is 1000 mL to 1100 mL. That is the expiratory reserve volume (ERV), the extra air that can be pushed out by forcible expiration. It is not the air that stays behind.\n500 mL is the tidal volume, and 2500 mL to 3000 mL is the inspiratory reserve volume (IRV).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A graph is plotted with the partial pressure of oxygen (0 to 100 mm Hg) on the X-axis and the percentage saturation of haemoglobin with oxygen on the Y-axis. What is the shape of the curve obtained, and what is it called?","image":"","options":["Hyperbolic; oxygen saturation curve","Sigmoid; oxygen dissociation curve","Linear; oxygen binding line","Bell-shaped; oxygen diffusion curve"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Plotting % saturation of haemoglobin against pO$_2$ gives a sigmoid (S-shaped) curve.\nNCERT calls this the oxygen dissociation curve.\nIt is useful for studying how pCO$_2$, H$^+$ concentration and similar factors affect O$_2$ binding to haemoglobin.\nThe curve is not linear, hyperbolic or bell-shaped.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Carbon dioxide bound to haemoglobin as carbamino-haemoglobin is released at the alveoli.\nReason (R): In the alveoli, pCO2 is low and pO2 is high, which favours dissociation of CO2 from carbamino-haemoglobin.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. CO2 picked up by haemoglobin at the tissues is delivered at the alveoli.\nR is true. Carbamino binding depends on pCO2, and pO2 is a major factor affecting it.\nHigh pCO2 with low pO2, as in the tissues, favours binding.\nLow pCO2 with high pO2, as in the alveoli, favours dissociation.\nR therefore directly explains why CO2 leaves haemoglobin at the alveoli.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the transport of gases and the regulation of respiration is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Neural signals from the pneumotaxic centre in the pons can shorten the duration of inspiration and thus alter the respiratory rate.","The chemosensitive area next to the respiratory rhythm centre is activated by an increase in CO2 and H+ concentration.","High pCO2 and low pO2 in the tissues favour the binding of CO2 to haemoglobin as carbamino-haemoglobin.","Carbonic anhydrase is present in very high concentration in plasma and only in minute quantities inside RBCs."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the pneumotaxic centre in the pons reduces the duration of inspiration and so changes the respiratory rate.\n(2) Correct: the chemosensitive area is highly sensitive to CO2 and H+, and an increase in either activates it.\n(3) Correct: high pCO2 and low pO2 in the tissues favour carbamino-haemoglobin formation.\n(4) Incorrect: the distribution is reversed. RBCs contain a very high concentration of carbonic anhydrase, while plasma has only minute amounts.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Larynx\n(b) Epiglottis\n(c) Pharynx\n(d) Alveoli\nList-II:\n(i) Common passage for food and air\n(ii) Thin, irregular-walled, vascularised bag-like structures\n(iii) Cartilaginous box helping in sound production\n(iv) Elastic cartilaginous flap covering the glottis\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Larynx: a cartilaginous box that helps in sound production, called the sound box, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Epiglottis: an elastic cartilaginous flap that covers the glottis during swallowing, so it matches (iv).\n(c) Pharynx: part of it is the common passage for food and air, so it matches (i).\n(d) Alveoli: thin, irregular-walled, vascularised sacs arising from terminal bronchioles, so they match (ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy man at rest breathes 15 times per minute. His tidal volume is 500 mL. What total volume of air does he inspire in one hour of normal breathing?","image":"","options":["7.5 L","450 L","360 L","480 L"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: tidal volume = 500 mL per breath, breathing rate = 15 breaths/min, time = 60 min.\nFormula: volume per hour = TV × breaths per minute × minutes.\nSubstituting: 500 × 15 = 7500 mL/min. This lies within the NCERT range of 6000 to 8000 mL/min.\nThen 7500 × 60 = 450000 mL.\nSo the volume inspired in one hour = 450 L. The option 7.5 L is only the volume for one minute.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about human respiratory volumes is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Tidal volume is the volume of air inspired or expired during a normal respiration and is about 500 mL","Expiratory reserve volume averages 2500 to 3000 mL","Residual volume averages 1100 to 1200 mL","A healthy human breathes on average 12 to 16 times per minute"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is the NCERT definition and value of tidal volume.\n(2) Incorrect: ERV averages 1000 to 1100 mL. The range 2500 to 3000 mL belongs to IRV.\n(3) Correct: RV averages 1100 to 1200 mL.\n(4) Correct: the normal breathing rate is 12 to 16 times per minute.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (organism) with List-II (means of gas exchange):\nList-I\nA. Earthworm\nB. Insects\nC. Fishes\nD. Coelenterates\nList-II\nI. Gills\nII. Simple diffusion over the entire body surface\nIII. Moist cuticle\nIV. Network of tracheal tubes\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-III: Earthworms exchange gases through their moist cuticle.\nB-IV: Insects carry atmospheric air inside the body through a network of tracheal tubes.\nC-I: Fishes respire through gills.\nD-II: Lower invertebrates such as coelenterates exchange gases by simple diffusion over the whole body surface.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In a patient, the diaphragm is paralysed but the external intercostal muscles work normally. Based on the mechanism of breathing, which prediction is most appropriate?","image":"","options":["Inspiration becomes impossible because the diaphragm is the only muscle that increases thoracic volume","Thoracic volume can still increase in the dorso-ventral axis, but the antero-posterior increase is lost","Thoracic volume can increase only in the antero-posterior axis","During inspiration, intra-pulmonary pressure would rise above atmospheric pressure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The diaphragm normally enlarges the thorax in the antero-posterior axis. This contribution is lost when the diaphragm is paralysed.\nThe external intercostals still lift the ribs and sternum, so the thorax can still enlarge in the dorso-ventral axis.\nThis partial increase in volume still lowers intra-pulmonary pressure below atmospheric pressure, so some inspiration is possible.\nOption 1 fails because the intercostals also enlarge the thorax. Option 4 describes the pressure change of expiration, not inspiration.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about respiratory mechanisms in animals is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Pulmonary respiration involves vascularised bags called lungs and is characteristic of terrestrial forms","The mechanism of breathing in an animal group depends mainly on its body size and type of diet","Insects carry atmospheric air within their body through a network of tracheal tubes","Flatworms have no special respiratory organ and exchange gases across the entire body surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: lungs are vascularised bags used by terrestrial forms.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT says breathing mechanisms vary mainly with the animal's habitat and level of organisation, not with body size or diet.\n(3) Correct: insects have tracheal tubes.\n(4) Correct: flatworms rely on simple diffusion over the body surface.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"During normal inspiration in humans, contraction of the diaphragm increases the volume of the thoracic chamber mainly along which axis?","image":"","options":["Dorso-ventral axis","Antero-posterior axis","Lateral (left-right) axis only","It does not change thoracic volume; it only lifts the ribs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Inspiration starts when the diaphragm contracts.\nThe dome-shaped diaphragm flattens, which enlarges the thoracic chamber along the antero-posterior axis.\nThe dorso-ventral increase is caused by a different muscle: the external intercostals, which lift the ribs and sternum. That makes option (1) the tempting wrong choice.\nLifting the ribs is not the diaphragm's job, so option (4) is also wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, approximately how much air can a healthy man inspire or expire per minute during normal breathing?","image":"","options":["500 to 600 mL","6000 to 8000 mL","2500 to 3000 mL","60,000 to 80,000 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Tidal volume is about 500 mL per breath.\nA healthy human breathes 12 to 16 times per minute.\nSo air moved per minute is about 500 × 12 to 16, which gives roughly 6000 to 8000 mL/min. This is the value NCERT states.\nThe 500 to 600 mL option is the volume of a single breath, not a minute's worth.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (respiratory volume) with List-II (average value in a healthy adult):\nList-I\nA. Tidal volume\nB. Inspiratory reserve volume\nC. Expiratory reserve volume\nD. Residual volume\nList-II\nI. 1000–1100 mL\nII. 2500–3000 mL\nIII. About 500 mL\nIV. 1100–1200 mL\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-III: Tidal volume is about 500 mL.\nB-II: IRV, the extra air taken in by forcible inspiration, is 2500–3000 mL.\nC-I: ERV, the extra air pushed out by forcible expiration, is 1000–1100 mL.\nD-IV: Residual volume, the air left in the lungs even after forcible expiration, is 1100–1200 mL.\nOption (1) is the tempting trap because it swaps ERV and RV.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following conditions favour the dissociation of oxygen from oxyhaemoglobin?\nHigh pO2, high pCO2, high H+ concentration, low temperature, high temperature, low pCO2","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Oxygen is released from oxyhaemoglobin under tissue conditions: low pO2, high pCO2, high H+ and higher temperature.\nFrom the list, high pCO2, high H+ and high temperature favour dissociation, which makes 3.\nHigh pO2, low pCO2 and low temperature are alveolar conditions. These favour the formation of oxyhaemoglobin, not its dissociation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, what is the partial pressure of oxygen in the alveoli?","image":"","options":["159 mm Hg","95 mm Hg","104 mm Hg","40 mm Hg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Table 14.1 lists $pO_2$ in alveoli as 104 mm Hg.\n159 mm Hg is atmospheric air.\n95 mm Hg is oxygenated blood.\n40 mm Hg is deoxygenated blood and tissues.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During swallowing, the glottis can be covered by the epiglottis.\nReason (R): The larynx is a bony box that helps in sound production.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The epiglottis is a thin, elastic, cartilaginous flap. It covers the glottis during swallowing and stops food from entering the larynx.\nR is false. The larynx is a cartilaginous box, not a bony one. It is called the sound box because it helps in sound production.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (animal) with List-II (respiratory surface or structure used for gas exchange).\nList-I:\n(A) Earthworm\n(B) Insects\n(C) Aquatic molluscs\n(D) Flatworms\nList-II:\n(i) Network of tracheal tubes\n(ii) Entire body surface by simple diffusion\n(iii) Moist cuticle\n(iv) Gills\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Earthworms exchange gases through their moist cuticle, so A-(iii).\n(B) Insects carry air inside the body through tracheal tubes, so B-(i).\n(C) Most aquatic arthropods and molluscs use vascularised gills, so C-(iv).\n(D) Lower invertebrates such as flatworms use simple diffusion over the whole body surface, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are part of the conducting part of the human respiratory system?\nExternal nostrils, pharynx, larynx, trachea, terminal bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveoli","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The conducting part extends from the external nostrils up to the terminal bronchioles.\nStructures in the list that fall in this stretch: external nostrils, pharynx, larynx, trachea and terminal bronchioles. That makes 5.\nAlveolar ducts and alveoli form the exchange part, so they are not counted.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose an injury breaks the air-tight nature of the thoracic chamber on one side. What would happen when the diaphragm contracts during an attempted inspiration?","image":"","options":["The thoracic volume would fail to increase because the diaphragm cannot contract","The lung on that side would expand more than usual because atmospheric air enters the thorax","The increase in thoracic volume would not be properly reflected in the lung on that side, so it would fail to draw in air effectively","Gas exchange would shift from the alveoli to the conducting part"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Normally the thorax is air-tight, so any rise in thoracic volume is passed on to the lungs. This lowers intra-pulmonary pressure below atmospheric pressure, and air flows in.\nIf the chamber is no longer air-tight, the diaphragm can still contract. However, the change in thoracic volume is no longer transmitted to that lung.\nPulmonary volume therefore does not rise properly, and inspiration on that side fails.\nThe conducting part never takes over gas exchange, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pulmonary capacity) with List-II (its components).\nList-I:\n(A) Inspiratory capacity\n(B) Expiratory capacity\n(C) Functional residual capacity\n(D) Vital capacity\nList-II:\n(i) ERV + RV\n(ii) TV + ERV\n(iii) TV + IRV\n(iv) ERV + TV + IRV\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Inspiratory capacity is the air a person can inspire after a normal expiration, which is TV + IRV, so A-(iii).\n(B) Expiratory capacity is the air a person can expire after a normal inspiration, which is TV + ERV, so B-(ii).\n(C) Functional residual capacity is the air left in the lungs after a normal expiration, which is ERV + RV, so C-(i).\n(D) Vital capacity is ERV + TV + IRV, so D-(iv).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (site) with List-II ($pO_2$ / $pCO_2$ in mm Hg) as given by NCERT.\nList-I\nA. Atmospheric air\nB. Alveoli\nC. Deoxygenated blood\nD. Oxygenated blood\nList-II\n(i) 95 / 40\n(ii) 159 / 0.3\n(iii) 104 / 40\n(iv) 40 / 45","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Atmospheric air: $pO_2$ 159, $pCO_2$ 0.3, so A matches (ii).\nB. Alveoli: $pO_2$ 104, $pCO_2$ 40, so B matches (iii).\nC. Deoxygenated blood: $pO_2$ 40, $pCO_2$ 45, so C matches (iv).\nD. Oxygenated blood: $pO_2$ 95, $pCO_2$ 40, so D matches (i).\nThe tempting mistake is to swap the two blood types. Deoxygenated blood has the lower $pO_2$ and the higher $pCO_2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the oxygen dissociation curve of haemoglobin. In actively contracting muscle, pO$_2$ stays the same but local conditions change. Which change would make haemoglobin show a LOWER percentage saturation at that pO$_2$, so that it releases more O$_2$ to the muscle?","image":"","options":["A fall in pCO$_2$ and a fall in temperature","A fall in H$^+$ concentration","A rise in the pO$_2$ of the surrounding tissue","A rise in H$^+$ concentration and a rise in temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Besides pO$_2$, the factors pCO$_2$, H$^+$ and temperature interfere with O$_2$ binding.\nHigh pCO$_2$, high H$^+$ and higher temperature, as found in tissues, favour dissociation of oxyhaemoglobin.\nSo at the same pO$_2$, saturation falls and more O$_2$ is released. Option (4) fits.\nFalls in pCO$_2$, H$^+$ or temperature mimic alveolar conditions and favour binding instead.\nA rise in pO$_2$ changes pO$_2$ itself, which the question holds constant.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about CO2 transport are correct?\n(i) At the tissues, CO2 entering the blood is converted into $HCO_3^-$ and $H^+$.\n(ii) Every 100 mL of deoxygenated blood delivers about 4 mL of CO2 to the alveoli.\n(iii) Carbonic anhydrase catalyses only the formation of $H_2CO_3$ from CO2 and water, not its breakdown.\n(iv) About 7 per cent of CO2 is carried in a dissolved state in plasma.\n(v) A high pO2 promotes binding of CO2 to haemoglobin.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: high pCO2 at the tissues drives the reaction towards $HCO_3^-$ and $H^+$.\n(ii) Correct: this is the NCERT value of about 4 mL per 100 mL of blood.\n(iii) Incorrect: the enzyme works in both directions. It reverses at the alveoli, forming CO2 and H2O.\n(iv) Correct: about 7% of CO2 is carried dissolved in plasma.\n(v) Incorrect: high pO2, as in the alveoli, favours dissociation of CO2 from haemoglobin.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pulmonary capacity) with List-II (components):\nList-I\nA. Inspiratory capacity\nB. Expiratory capacity\nC. Functional residual capacity\nD. Total lung capacity\nList-II\n(i) ERV + RV\n(ii) TV + IRV\n(iii) VC + RV\n(iv) TV + ERV\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. IC is the air inspired after a normal expiration, so IC = TV + IRV: (ii).\nB. EC is the air expired after a normal inspiration, so EC = TV + ERV: (iv).\nC. FRC is the air left after a normal expiration, so FRC = ERV + RV: (i).\nD. TLC is the air in the lungs after a forced inspiration, so TLC = VC + RV: (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The larynx is commonly called the sound box.\nReason (R): The larynx is a bony chamber located above the pharynx.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The larynx helps in sound production, so it is called the sound box.\nR is false on two counts. The larynx is cartilaginous, not bony.\nIt also lies below the pharynx, because the pharynx opens through the larynx into the trachea.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A sample is found to have $pO_2$ = 104 mm Hg and $pCO_2$ = 40 mm Hg. According to NCERT data, this sample most likely represents:","image":"","options":["Atmospheric air","Deoxygenated blood","Body tissues","Alveolar air"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT gives $pO_2$/$pCO_2$ values (mm Hg) at each site:\nAtmospheric air = 159/0.3\nAlveoli = 104/40\nDeoxygenated blood = 40/45\nOxygenated blood = 95/40\nTissues = 40/45\nOnly alveolar air matches 104/40. Oxygenated blood also has a $pCO_2$ of 40, but its $pO_2$ is 95, not 104.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the alveolar diffusion membrane are correct?\n(i) It is made up of three major layers.\n(ii) Its total thickness is much less than a millimetre.\n(iii) Its basement substance includes the basement membrane of the alveolar epithelium and that of the capillary endothelium.\n(iv) The capillary endothelium is made of several layers of cells.\n(v) The alveolar epithelium is thin squamous epithelium.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the membrane has three major layers.\n(ii) Correct: its total thickness is much less than a millimetre.\n(iii) Correct: the basement substance contains both basement membranes.\n(iv) Incorrect: the capillary endothelium is a single layer of cells.\n(v) Correct: the alveolar epithelium is thin squamous epithelium.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although the pharynx is a common passage for food and air, food normally does not enter the larynx during swallowing.\nReason (R): During swallowing, the glottis can be covered by the epiglottis, a thin elastic cartilaginous flap.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Part of the pharynx is shared by food and air, yet food is kept out of the larynx.\nR is true. The epiglottis is a thin elastic cartilaginous flap that covers the glottis during swallowing.\nCovering the glottis is exactly what stops food from entering the larynx, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"The term 'branchial respiration' refers to exchange of gases through:","image":"","options":["Gills","Lungs","Moist skin","Tracheal tubes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT calls respiration through special vascularised gills 'branchial respiration'.\nIt is used by most aquatic arthropods, molluscs and fishes.\nRespiration through lungs is pulmonary respiration, and respiration through skin is cutaneous respiration.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the same difference in partial pressure, much more $CO_2$ than $O_2$ diffuses across the respiratory membrane.\nReason (R): The solubility of $CO_2$ is 20–25 times higher than that of $O_2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: NCERT states that $CO_2$ is 20–25 times more soluble than $O_2$.\nA is true: because of this higher solubility, the amount of $CO_2$ that diffuses per unit difference in partial pressure is much higher than that of $O_2$.\nR directly explains A, since solubility is one of the factors that sets the rate of diffusion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Nearly what percentage of carbon dioxide is transported in blood in the form of bicarbonate ions?","image":"","options":["About 7 per cent","About 20-25 per cent","About 70 per cent","About 97 per cent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that nearly 70 per cent of $CO_2$ is carried as bicarbonate ($HCO_3^-$).\nAbout 20-25 per cent travels bound to haemoglobin as carbamino-haemoglobin.\nAbout 7 per cent is dissolved in plasma.\nThe figure 97 per cent is the share of oxygen carried by RBCs, not $CO_2$ as bicarbonate.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A spirometer test on a person gives the following values:\nTidal volume = 500 mL\nInspiratory reserve volume = 2500 mL\nExpiratory reserve volume = 1000 mL\nResidual volume = 1100 mL\nWhat are this person's functional residual capacity (FRC) and inspiratory capacity (IC), respectively?","image":"","options":["2100 mL and 3000 mL","1600 mL and 3000 mL","2100 mL and 3500 mL","1500 mL and 4000 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"FRC is the air left in the lungs after a normal expiration, so FRC = ERV + RV = 1000 + 1100 = 2100 mL.\nIC is the air a person can inspire after a normal expiration, so IC = TV + IRV = 500 + 2500 = 3000 mL.\nThe value 1600 mL wrongly adds TV + RV. The value 3500 mL wrongly adds IRV + ERV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"A healthy person at rest breathes 15 times per minute with a tidal volume of 500 mL. What volume of air does this person inspire in one minute?","image":"","options":["6000 mL","750 mL","7500 mL","8000 mL"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: breathing rate = 15 per minute, tidal volume = 500 mL.\nFormula: volume per minute = tidal volume × breathing rate.\nSubstituting: 500 mL × 15 = 7500 mL.\nSo the volume inspired per minute = 7500 mL. This fits NCERT's range of 6000-8000 mL per minute.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In the human respiratory system, the two lungs are covered by a double-layered pleura with pleural fluid between the layers. What is the main role of this pleural fluid?","image":"","options":["It humidifies the inspired air before it reaches the alveoli","It reduces friction on the lung surface","It traps dust particles entering the bronchi","It serves as the site of exchange of $O_2$ and $CO_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the lungs are covered by a double-layered pleura with pleural fluid between the layers.\nThis fluid reduces friction on the lung surface as the lungs move during breathing.\nHumidifying the air and clearing foreign particles are jobs of the conducting part, not the pleura.\nGas exchange occurs only in the alveoli.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the alveoli, conditions favour the formation of oxyhaemoglobin.\nReason (R): The alveoli have high pO$_2$, low pCO$_2$, lesser H$^+$ concentration and lower temperature compared to the tissues.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. O$_2$ binds to haemoglobin at the lung surface, forming oxyhaemoglobin.\nR is true. NCERT lists high pO$_2$, low pCO$_2$, lesser H$^+$ and lower temperature as the conditions in the alveoli.\nThese are exactly the factors that favour O$_2$ binding to haemoglobin, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"What is the main function of the epiglottis?","image":"","options":["Producing sound during speech","Covering the glottis during swallowing so that food does not enter the larynx","Humidifying and warming the inhaled air","Preventing collapse of the trachea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The epiglottis is a thin, elastic, cartilaginous flap.\nDuring swallowing it covers the glottis, which stops food from entering the larynx.\nSound production is the job of the larynx.\nThe trachea is kept open by incomplete cartilaginous rings, not by the epiglottis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Contraction of the diaphragm initiates inspiration.\nReason (R): Contraction of the diaphragm increases the volume of the thoracic chamber in the antero-posterior axis, which lowers the intra-pulmonary pressure below atmospheric pressure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that inspiration is initiated by contraction of the diaphragm.\nR is true. The contracting diaphragm enlarges the thorax in the antero-posterior axis. Pulmonary volume increases with it, so intra-pulmonary pressure falls below atmospheric pressure.\nThis pressure drop is what draws air into the lungs, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of the human respiratory tract is a cartilaginous box that helps in sound production?","image":"","options":["Pharynx","Larynx","Trachea","Epiglottis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The larynx is a cartilaginous box between the pharynx and the trachea.\nBecause it helps in producing sound, it is called the sound box.\nThe epiglottis is only a cartilaginous flap that covers the glottis. It is not the box itself.\nThe pharynx and the trachea do not produce sound.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The amount of $CO_2$ that diffuses through the diffusion membrane per unit difference in partial pressure is much higher than that of $O_2$.\nReason (R): $CO_2$ is 20-25 times more soluble than $O_2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that $CO_2$ diffuses in much larger amounts per unit pressure difference.\nR is true: the solubility of $CO_2$ is 20-25 times that of $O_2$.\nNCERT attributes the greater diffusion directly to this higher solubility, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pulmonary capacity) with List-II (its components) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\n(a) Inspiratory capacity\n(b) Expiratory capacity\n(c) Functional residual capacity\n(d) Total lung capacity\nList-II:\n(i) TV + ERV\n(ii) VC + RV\n(iii) TV + IRV\n(iv) ERV + RV","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Inspiratory capacity is the total air inspired after a normal expiration, so it is TV + IRV → (iii).\n(b) Expiratory capacity is the total air expired after a normal inspiration, so it is TV + ERV → (i).\n(c) Functional residual capacity is the air left after a normal expiration, so it is ERV + RV → (iv).\n(d) Total lung capacity is the air held at the end of a forced inspiration, so it is VC + RV → (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The $pO_2$ gradient from alveoli to deoxygenated blood is roughly 13 times the $pCO_2$ gradient from deoxygenated blood to alveoli.\nStatement II: Despite its smaller partial pressure gradient, $CO_2$ is efficiently removed at the alveoli because it is much more soluble than $O_2$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The $O_2$ gradient is 104 − 40 = 64 mm Hg.\nThe $CO_2$ gradient is 45 − 40 = 5 mm Hg.\nThe ratio is 64/5 ≈ 12.8, which is about 13, so Statement I is correct.\n$CO_2$ is 20-25 times more soluble than $O_2$, so much more diffuses per unit pressure difference.\nThis makes up for its small gradient, so Statement II is also correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following relations among pulmonary volumes and capacities is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["VC = TLC − RV","FRC = EC − TV + RV","IC = VC − FRC","TLC = IC + FRC"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: TLC − RV = ERV + TV + IRV = VC.\n(2) Correct: EC − TV + RV = (TV + ERV) − TV + RV = ERV + RV = FRC.\n(3) Incorrect: VC − FRC = (ERV + TV + IRV) − (ERV + RV) = TV + IRV − RV. This is IC − RV, not IC.\n(4) Correct: IC + FRC = TV + IRV + ERV + RV = TLC.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How does the solubility of $CO_2$ compare with that of $O_2$?","image":"","options":["About 2-3 times higher","About 20-25 times higher","About 20-25 times lower","Nearly equal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the solubility of $CO_2$ is 20-25 times higher than that of $O_2$.\nBecause of this, much more $CO_2$ diffuses per unit difference in partial pressure.\nThis is why $CO_2$ diffuses efficiently even though its partial pressure gradient is small.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"In humans, the trachea divides into the right and left primary bronchi at the level of the:","image":"","options":["3rd thoracic vertebra","7th cervical vertebra","5th thoracic vertebra","1st lumbar vertebra"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The trachea is a straight tube that extends up to the mid-thoracic cavity.\nIt divides into the right and left primary bronchi at the level of the 5th thoracic vertebra.\nCervical and lumbar levels lie outside the mid-thoracic region, so they do not fit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-BREATHING-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Breathing and Exchange of Gases","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the regulation of respiration are correct?\n(i) The respiratory rhythm centre is located in the medulla.\n(ii) The pneumotaxic centre in the pons can reduce the duration of inspiration.\n(iii) The chemosensitive area is mainly sensitive to a fall in $O_2$ concentration.\n(iv) Receptors in the aortic arch and carotid artery can detect changes in $CO_2$ and $H^+$ concentration.\n(v) Oxygen plays an insignificant role in regulating respiratory rhythm.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the rhythm centre lies in the medulla.\n(ii) Correct: the pneumotaxic centre shortens inspiration and so alters the respiratory rate.\n(iii) Incorrect: the chemosensitive area responds to $CO_2$ and $H^+$, not to $O_2$.\n(iv) Correct: receptors in the aortic arch and carotid artery detect $CO_2$ and $H^+$ changes.\n(v) Correct: NCERT states that the role of $O_2$ is quite insignificant.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 14 · Breathing and Exchange of Gases","difficulty":"medium"}]});
